{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": "# Implementation\n\n- Changed only `rtl/reg_file.sv` and `test/test_reg_file.py`.\n- Applied Gowin's `syn_ramstyle=\"registers\"` attribute to the existing\n  32-word array. Kept the synchronous all-zero reset, reset-over-write\n  priority, single nonzero-address write, complete interface, and ID/EX\n  boundary.\n- Generated one-hot read selects for x1 through x31 and a per-word write\n  select shared by both ports and the synchronous storage updates. Each\n  stored contribution is masked by its\n  read select AND NOT its local write select. Each ascending procedural\n  OR reduction starts with the nonzero-address write-first bypass term.\n  x0 does not participate in either reduction.\n- Combinational bypass remains active during reset, exactly as in the\n  original module. An enabled write coincident with a reset edge clears\n  storage; disabling bypass then exposes zeros.\n- Retained all five existing register-file tests. Added exhaustive stored\n  read-address pairs, every nonzero address with both-port and either-port\n  bypass, complementary overwrites to expose stale-word OR contributions,\n  disabled writes, x0 writes, repeated resets of dirty storage coincident\n  with enabled writes, first writes after reset, and 2,048 deterministic\n  randomized reference-model cycles including consecutive resets.\n\nThe one local deviation is replacing the original indexed storage write\nwith an ascending loop using those same per-word write selects. Only one\nnonzero word can update, with the original reset priority. This version\nreduced total mapped logic and improved median Fmax from 53.507 MHz for\nthe indexed-write implementation to 55.932 MHz. Both implementations\npassed the complete formal suite. No architectural changes: pipeline\ntiming, forwarding, multiply and divider behavior, memory handshakes,\ntraps, and the nret=1 RVFI interface were left unchanged.\n\n# Local validation\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings.\n- Full bench cocotb suite: PASS, all 13 pytest runners, including all eight\n  register-file cocotb tests (five\n…[1235 of 4307 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:aa5cb9fa636c98e7]…\nhesis resource XML confirms `cpu/u_rf` uses 992 flops, 1,511 LUTs,\n10 ALU cells, and no BSRAM/SSRAM. The four BSRAMs belong to the benchmark\nwrapper's memories. The device report lists one MULT18X18 and three\nMULT36X36 primitives (6.5 DSP units).\n\nMedian Fmax is 55.932 MHz; measured fitness is 133.12 iterations/second.\nThe required ratio is\n`(55.932 / 58.252) * (4201709 / 4201709) = 0.9601730413`, which is below\n1.046. This implementation is a measured regression, not an accepted win.\n\nThe worst option-0 path starts at `cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0`, crosses\nforwarding/EX operand selection and the ALU, and ends at a timed data-memory\ninput. Its reported arrival is 24.387 ns across 17 cells. Options 1 and 2\nare likewise limited by forwarding/EX operand selection and the ALU into\ntimed memory, with 23.173 ns reported arrival across 19 cells. Branch\npredictor training is another nearby path. The increased flop/LUT area\naccompany the regression; eliminating register-file BSRAMs did not yield\nthe intended timing gain.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
