{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_ex_divider.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Operand delivery retiming\n\nAdded `alu_a_val` and `alu_b_val` at the high end of ID/EX. ID captures the\nPC/rs1 and immediate/rs2 choices using the existing write-first register file.\nThe original sources, addresses, PC, controls, and prediction metadata remain.\n\nThe forwarding unit now computes next-cycle near/far matches in ID against\ncurrent ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations, using valid post-trap write indications\nexported from EX and MEM. It registers three-way one-hot selections on exactly\nthe ID/EX reset/flush/stall/capture schedule. Near matches take priority and x0\nnever forwards. EX uses masked ORs for architectural sources and prepared ALU\noperands; PC/immediate operands suppress register bypass masks. The ordinary\nALU and divider launch use the prepared operands, while branches, stores, JALR,\nmultiply prediction, and RVFI use the architectural sources.\n\nNo architectural deviations: pipeline depth, instruction latencies, issue\nrate, RF module, decoder, arithmetic/ALTOPS formulas, branch prediction,\niterative divider, exact MEM multiplication validation, and retirement wiring\nare preserved, including top-level I/O and `nret: 1`. The only additional test\nadaptation is the EX divider unit test's input bundle and new one-hot encodings,\nalongside bounded packed PC extraction in the pipeline test.\n\nAll existing pipeline assertions remain. Five directed cases add simultaneous\nnear/far writers, both-source matches, x0, raw U/I-field collisions, trapped\nJAL/JALR/load write suppression, a load-use release, retained far WB load data\nthrough seven store-hold cycles, and bubble-free divider-result forwarding.\nThe shared harness also checks selection and prepared-operand capture/hold/\nflush alignment throughout the existing branch/division/multiply repair cases.\n\nLocal checks:\n\n- Required Verilator lint: pass, no warnings.\n- Complete cocotb suite: 13 pytest runners passed; the final pipeline runner\n  passes all 28 cocotb cases, including all 23 existing cases.\n- Final full local formal: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed. The original equivalent\n  priority-encoded selection version also passed all 53 checks. Neither run\n  produced a counterexample; no correctness fix attempts were needed.\n- Cosimulation: pass for selftest with and without random memory stalls and\n  the CoreMark output validator.\n- CoreMark IPC validator: ten stalled iterations, exactly 4,201,709 bracketed\n  cycles, canonical CRCs and validation literal, no out-of-bounds access.\n\nThe current source expresses each one-hot bit explicitly. Unchanged Gowin\nplacement options measure 63.388 / 65.311 / 65.311 MHz, median 65.311 MHz.\nOption zero uses 3,791 logic cells, 970 registers, 40 LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs,\nand six reported DSPs. With unchanged 4,201,709 cycles, fitness is 155.44\niterations/s and the acceptance ratio is approximately 1.1212, above 1.046.\nThis is slightly below the hypothesis's isolated 65.936 MHz measurement.\nThe initial equivalent priority-encoded selection mapping gave a 59.817 MHz\nmedian; expressing the masks individually improved vendor mapping without\nchanging their behavior. Full formal, pipeline tests, and cosimulation against\nthe final mapping all pass.\n\nThe remaining critical cone is MEM exact multiplication validation/recovery,\nrather than the register-file output. Physical mapping remains sensitive to\nthe one-hot logic expression. Full real-arithmetic formal-deep was not run;\nreal M-extension unit tests and cosimulation passed.\n","exit