{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id']=='hyp-20261005-002-r9s1':\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replace the cascaded OC forwarding, retained-source and ALU-role muxes\nwith one role-specific capture crossbar feeding the existing OC/EX\noperand registers. Treat the independently retained rs1/rs2 values as\nfirst-class crossbar inputs, alongside current EX return, registered\nMEM result, WB value and ID snapshot. Treat PC and immediate as parallel\ncrossbar inputs for prepared ALU roles. Build separate capture outputs\nfor architectural rs1, architectural rs2, ALU a and ALU b directly from\nmutually exclusive control masks. The data path is candidate bus ->\nmask/balanced OR -> existing OC/EX register. No output of a complete\nforwarding mux may then pass through a retained-source mux or an\nimmediate/PC mux. No source-selection mux moves after OC/EX into EX.\nThis is one structural replacement of the operand-capture topology,\nnot an additional pipeline stage, arithmetic approximation or new\npredictor/memory policy.\n\nFor each source, a saved-ready value has priority and suppresses every\nlive producer route; otherwise x0 selects zero, followed by the nearest\nqualified EX writer, then MEM, then WB, then the ID snapshot. Qualify\nevery writer exactly as today: valid, nonzero destination and final\npost-trap write eligibility, with older repair suppressing younger work.\nSelect producer identity independently of readiness. An unavailable\nnearest EX load/divider or MEM load must block capture instead of\nexposing an older same-rd value. Generate explicit mutually exclusive\none-hot masks, rather than an encoded route followed by another decoder.\nFor ALU a, AND all architectural route masks with !is_auipc and add\nPC masked by is_auipc as a parallel term. For ALU b, AND source masks\nwith !alu_src and add immediate masked by alu_src. Architectural RVFI\nsource capture remains independent of these ALU operand roles.\n\nPreserve OC's per-source ready/value retention and exact advance,\noccupied-EX hold and flush priorities. The ordinary resolved-source\npath may remain for capturing a newly ready operand into OC retention\nduring a wait, bu\n…[1079 of 4151 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ed6c6e72ec521ef3]…\n MEM/WB\nretirement, decoder/trap rules, memory alignment/side-effect checks,\nRVFI source values and strictly consecutive retirement order. No\nevaluation-contract path changes are proposed.\n\nValidate every source combination, saved-source priority, same-rd\nproducer shadowing, x0, architectural versus ALU roles, captured source\nretention and both recovery causes. Retain existing full-rate ALU and\nsingle-bubble load-use timing assertions. Run unchanged formal,\nready/stalled trace cosim, canonical CoreMark CRC/validation literal\nand MMIO brackets, and all three vendor Gowin placement options.\nAcceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 85.344) * (4,196,601 / cycles_new) > 1.046;\nwith identical scheduling the median Fmax must exceed 89.270 MHz.\nThe expected gain is an estimate. Main risks are synthesis refactoring\nthe crossbar back into serial muxes, mask fanout, and another EX control\npath becoming dominant. Preliminary measurements will be recorded below;\nthey do not substitute for implementation-phase correctness gates.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
