{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwanted={'hyp-20261005-001-r9s0','hyp-20261005-001-r11s0','hyp-20261005-002-r14s1','hyp-20261005-001-r5s0','hyp-20261005-001-r12s0','hyp-20261005-001-r10s0'}\nfor s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d['id'] in wanted:\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-001-r5s0\nImplement the existing 32x32 register file as resettable flops with\nexplicit one-hot asynchronous read selection. Apply Gowin's supported\nsyn_ramstyle=\"registers\" attribute to the data array, preserving the\noriginal synchronous all-zero reset and single nonzero-register write.\nFor each register i=1..31, form write_i = w_en && w_addr==i and two\nread selects read1_i = rs1_addr==i and read2_i = rs2_addr==i. Share\nwrite_i across both read ports. The stored-word contribution on port k\nis regs[i] AND replicated(readk_i && !write_i). Thus a word being\nwritten never contributes stale data, with suppression derived from\nthe local decoded write address rather than a high-fanout global\nread/write match. Never include x0 in the stored-word reduction.\n\nForm each read result as the OR of these masked words and a bypass\ncandidate w_data AND replicated(w_en && w_addr!=0 && w_addr==rsK_addr).\nIn a procedural reduction, initialize the accumulator to this bypass\ncandidate and then OR registers 1 through 31 in ascending order.\nExactly one stored word or the bypass candidate may contribute.\nThis removes the separate wide write-first mux after the register\narray read while retaining exact same-cycle behavior on either or\nboth ports. Use ordinary synthesizable SystemVerilog, not FPGA\nprimitives, so formal and simulation see the same storage and masks.\nKeep the complete existing reg_file interface and ID/EX boundary.\n\nThis is one local physical implementation change to register-file\nreads. Keep instruction timing, forwarding priority, source snapshots,\nmultiply prediction and repair, divider transactions, branch handling,\nmemory handshakes, trap discipline, RVFI ordering and nret=1 unchanged.\nThe register file must remain zero after every reset, including a reset\ncoincident with an enabled write; subsequent combinational bypass must\nretain the original module's behavior. In particular, reads of x0\nalways return zero, writes to x0 never alter storage, and both read\nports observe a completed write coherently.\n\nThe me\n…[25295 of 28367 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c5c8200419960733]…\npending saved sources. Reset initializes MEM/WB and\nreference validity; invalid or flushed references have no effects.\nPreserve default-illegal decoding, SYSTEM policy, real and ALTOPS\narithmetic, nret=1, all external ports, original RVFI addresses/data,\nmemory records, PC successors and consecutive retirement order.\n\nThe expected eight-percent fitness gain has low confidence. Scheduling\nshould retain the champion's load-use and normal dependent-ALU timing,\nbut the added EX bank mux may make arithmetic or recovery the new\nlimit. Require (Fmax_new/91.186)*(4196601/cycles_new) > 1.046 using\nunchanged CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket validation and all three Gowin\nplacements. At identical cycles, acceptance needs median Fmax above\n95.381 MHz; eight percent needs about 98.481 MHz. These are targets,\nnot measured results. Run unchanged formal, all affected unit suites\nand ready/stalled field-by-field trace cosim. Do not alter any evaluator,\nwrapper, benchmark, stall generator, formal bound or another core.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
