{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_89","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m focusing the proposal on the register-file read circuit, the strongest result so far. The final refinement uses local write enables to suppress stored words during same-cycle bypass, so write data can join the one-hot read result directly. I’m checking its timing and reset/bypass behavior before updating the YAML."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_90", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r5s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261005-001-r5s0\ntitle: \\\"Use a flop register file with word-local one-hot bypass reads\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 138.64 at 58.252 MHz with 4,201,709 measured\n  CoreMark cycles. A fresh Gowin timing report confirms that its worst\n  path starts at a register-file BSRAM, whose clock-to-output delay is\n  2.260 ns, then crosses operand selection and the ALU before reaching\n  the timed memory interface. Earlier decode operand preselection\n  improved its then-parent but did not beat this champion; repeating it\n  on the current RTL also failed. A distributed-RAM register-file probe\n  removed the two register-file BSRAMs but reached only 57.741 MHz.\n  A flat one-hot flop register file instead reached 59.880 / 60.833 /\n  60.833 MHz across the three vendor placement options and passed all\n  five existing register-file tests. Its 4.43 percent frequency gain is\n  just below acceptance. This proposal refines that same register-file\n  read implementation by folding write-first bypass into word-local\n  selection, rather than adding another architectural feature.\nhypothesis: |\n  Implement the existing 32x32 register file as resettable flops with\n  explicit one-hot asynchronous read selection. Apply Gowin's supported\n  syn_ramstyle=\\\"registers\\\" attribute to the data array, preserving the\n  original synchronous all-zero reset and single nonzero-register write.\n  For each register i=1..31, form write_i = w_en && w_addr==i and two\n  read selects read1_i = rs1_addr==i and read2_i = rs2_addr==i. Share\n  write_i across both read ports. The stored-word contribution on port k\n  is regs[i] AND replicated(readk_i && \"'!write_i). Thus a word being\n  written never contributes stale data, with suppression derived from\n  the local decoded write address rather than a high-fanout global\n  read/write match. Never include x0 in the stored-word reduction.\n\n  Form each read result as the OR of these masked words and a bypass\n  candidate w_data AND replicated(w_en && w_addr!=0 && w_addr==rsK_addr).\n  In a procedural reduction, initialize the accumulator to this bypass\n  candidate and then OR registers 1 through 31 in ascending order.\n  Exactly one stored word or the bypass candidate may contribute.\n  This removes the separate wide write-first mux after the register\n  array read while retaining exact same-cycle behavior on either or\n  both ports. Use ordinary synthesizable SystemVerilog, not FPGA\n  primitives, so formal and simulation see the same storage and masks.\n  Keep the complete existing reg_file interface and ID/EX boundary.\n\n  This is one local physical implementation change to register-file\n  reads. Keep instruction timing, forwarding priority, source snapshots,\n  multiply prediction and repair, divider transactions, branch handling,\n  memory handshakes, trap discipline, RVFI ordering and nret=1 unchanged.\n  The register file must remain zero after every reset, including a reset\n  coincident with an enabled write; subsequent combinational bypass must\n  retain the original module'\"'s behavior. In particular, reads of x0\n  always return zero, writes to x0 never alter storage, and both read\n  ports observe a completed write coherently.\n\n  The measured flat-selector probe is evidence for the physical storage\n  angle, not a claimed accepted improvement: 60.833 MHz alone misses\n  the 60.932 MHz threshold. The local bypass refinement is intended to\n  remove remaining read selection depth and routing pressure sufficiently\n  to exceed that threshold with the same 4,201,709 CoreMark cycles.\n  Principal risks are extra flop/LUT area and an exposed multiply-repair\n  or forwarding-control critical path. Confirm the actual mapped\n  resources and all three Gowin placement options. Keep every existing\n  correctness gate and require (Fmax_new/58.252)*(4201709/cycles_new)>1.046;\n  do not claim a win from eliminating BSRAMs or from unit tests alone.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Force resettable flop storage using the vendor-supported registers\n      RAM-style attribute. Decode read addresses one-hot and fold bypass\n      into the masked-word OR reduction using shared per-word write\n      enables. Preserve synchronous reset priority, two combinational\n      ports, exact write-first semantics, x0, and all existing ports.\n      Inspect Gowin inference and timing instead of assuming that the\n      RTL attribute or selector shape improved frequency.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing tests. Add deterministic randomized write/read\n      checks against an all-zero-reset reference model, both-port bypass,\n      bypass on one port while the other reads a different stored word,\n      every register address, x0 writes, and repeated resets after data\n      have been written. Check reset coincident with write enable and\n      first writes after reset to detect stale-word OR contributions.\n''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261005-001-r5s0' and h['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Refined hypothesis written and schema validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
