{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '88,155p' tools/eval/formal.py; sed -n '1,100p' formal/run_all.sh; cat tools/eval/_slots.py\"", "aggregated_output": "def _cleanup_formal_workdir(worktree_path: Path, core_name: str | None,\n                            work_id: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Remove this run's riscv-formal work dir once the tally is parsed.\n\n    run_formal() pins the dir name through FORMAL_WORK_ID, so this removes\n    exactly `formal/riscv-formal/cores/<CORE_NAME>-<work_id>` and nothing\n    else: never a concurrent agent self-check's dir (formal/run_all.sh\n    names those with mktemp and reaps its own leftovers by flock). An SBY\n    work dir runs to 100+ MB, one per slot eval, so it must not be left.\n\n    Guard: BENCH_KEEP_FORMAL_WORKDIR=1 skips cleanup entirely (debugging).\n    formal/last_run-<work_id>.log is kept either way.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not core_name or os.environ.get(\"BENCH_KEEP_FORMAL_WORKDIR\") == \"1\":\n        return\n    shutil.rmtree(worktree_path / \"formal\" / \"riscv-formal\" / \"cores\"\n                  / f\"{core_name}-{work_id}\", ignore_errors=True)\n\n\ndef run_formal(worktree: str, target: str | None = None) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"\n    Args:\n      worktree: path to the repo root.\n      target:   optional core name (e.g. 'v1').  When set, injects\n                RTL_DIR=cores/<target>/rtl and CORE_NAME=<target> into\n                the subprocess environment so run_all.sh picks up that\n                core's RTL instead of the default rtl/ directory. When\n                that core's core.yaml declares nret=1, also injects\n                WRAPPER=formal/wrapper_si.sv and CHECKS_CFG=formal/checks_si.cfg\n                so the single-issue formal flow runs instead of the\n                default dual-channel one.\n\n    Returns:\n      {'passed': True, 'checks_passed': N}\n      {'passed': False, 'failed_check': name, 'detail': str}\n    \"\"\"\n    worktree_path = Path(worktree).resolve()\n    run_script = worktree_path / \"formal\" / \"run_all.sh\"\n    if not run_script.exists():\n        return {'passed': False, 'failed_check': 'setup',\n                'detail': f'formal/run_all.sh missing in {worktree}'}\n\n    env = _build_formal_env(worktree_path, target)\n    # Pin the w\n…[6900 of 9972 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0f4bd125385b5cd1]…\n   d = Path(lock_dir)\n    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    started, told = time.time(), False\n    while True:\n        for i in range(n):\n            f = open(d / f\"slot{i}.lock\", \"w\")\n            try:\n                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n            except BlockingIOError:\n                f.close()\n                continue\n            waited = time.time() - started\n            if waited > 60:\n                print(f\"  [eval] {what}: got host eval slot {i} after {waited:.0f}s\", flush=True)\n            try:\n                yield\n            finally:\n                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n                f.close()\n            return\n        if not told:\n            print(f\"  [eval] {what}: waiting for a host eval slot ({n} busy)\", flush=True)\n            told = True\n        time.sleep(5)\n\n\ndef eval_jobs(default: str | None = None) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"make -j for a formal run holding a slot (HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS).\"\"\"\n    return os.environ.get(\"HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS\") or default\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"agent_message","text":"The latest probe has a promising result: an operand-range check removes the full product comparison from recovery control. Vendor median Fmax rose from 65.311 to 72.022 MHz, while stalled CoreMark retained exactly 4,201,709 measured cycles and passed CRC validation. The core still writes the exact product; operands outside the proven ranges conservatively replay the younger instruction."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r6s0',\n'title':'Use operand-range certificates to shorten multiply recovery',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 155.44 at median Fmax 65.311 MHz with 4,201,709\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Its 21.071 ns, 33-cell worst path starts in\nMEM's exact multiplier and reaches younger EX and memory outputs through\nthe exact-versus-predicted product comparison and recovery. The existing\npredictor already has a simple sufficient correctness condition: MUL is\nexact when both original operands are sign-extended 16-bit values. That\ncondition can be checked without waiting for a DSP product. LESSONS.md\nis absent; relevant micro_opt and multiplication attempts in log.jsonl\nwere reviewed. Registered recovery regressed, so shortening recovery's\ncombinational predicate while retaining its current cycle is preferable.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace MEM's full-width exact-product-versus-predicted-product equality\non the recovery control path with a cheap, sufficient correctness\ncertificate derived from the original registered operands and operation.\nThis optimizes validation of the existing multiply predictor; it adds no\npredictor, execution stage or delayed recovery. Keep the exact multiplier\nunchanged and always send its true result to MEM/WB for architectural\nwriteback, whether the certificate succeeds or fails.\n\nIn real-arithmetic builds, certify any multiplication when either full\n32-bit operand is zero. Otherwise, certify ALU_MUL only when each operand\nhas bits [31:16] equal to sixteen copies of its own bit 15. Under that\ncondition the original operands equal the signed 16-bit inputs used by\nmul_predictor, so the low-32-bit prediction is exact. For MULH, MULHU and\nMULHSU, whose existing prediction is zero, certify when both original\noperands have upper 16 bits zero. Both are then nonnegative and their\nproduct is below 2\"'^32, so every high-product variant returns zero.\nUse bitwise equality/reduction logic, not multiplication, subtraction,\na stored prediction table or a fallback exact-product comparison.\n\nFor an advancing valid legal multiplication writing a nonzero rd,\nrepair whenever the certificate is false, even if the predicted value\nhappens to equal the exact result. The certificate is sufficient, not\nnecessary: false negatives cause safe additional replays, never wrong\nresults. For example, MUL of 0x00010000 by 1 has an incorrect low-half\nprediction and must replay; MUL of 0x00010000 by 0x00010000 has a matching\nzero prediction but may conservatively replay. A zero operand is always\ncertified regardless of the other operand'\"'s width. Uncertified operands\nremain fully supported; never trap, truncate architectural operands,\ncommit an unvalidated product, or assume a benchmark-specific range.\n\nKeep all existing recovery qualification and priority: reset and MEM\nholds suppress repair, invalid/illegal/nonwriting/x0 payloads do not\nrepair, and a repair uses the saved pc_next. The older multiplication\nretires once with its exact product and original RVFI sources. On that\nsame cycle, squash younger EX, flush ID, redirect fetch and suppress\nits divider launch/wait, branch redirect and predictor training exactly\nas today. Correctly certified operations keep the existing latency and\nnear bypass. Leave register-file, operand forwarding, all pipeline data\nclearing, branch prediction, decoder, bus handshakes and iterative divider\nunchanged. Preserve nret=1, top-level ports and all hard invariants.\n\nUnder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS the existing predictor and exact multiplier\nuse identical operation-specific add/sub-XOR formulas on identical\noriginal operands. Certify every multiply in that build, preserving\nthose formulas unchanged in both helpers and the standalone ALU.\nDo not apply real signed-16-bit range assumptions to ALTOPS arithmetic.\nReal-arithmetic tests must cover both true prediction failures and\nunnecessary but safe replays, since the ordinary formal gate substitutes\nmultiplication and cannot prove the real certificate's arithmetic fact.\n\nAn isolated probe changing only mem_stage.sv's recovery predicate has\ncompleted the unchanged Gowin placement options at 72.526, 72.022 and\n72.022 MHz, median 72.022 MHz. Option zero uses 3,682 logic cells,\n1,002 registers, 40 LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs and six DSPs. Its worst timing\npath now traverses the existing EX narrow multiplier instead of MEM's\nexact product/comparison. The existing complete pipeline test runner\npasses, including real product variants, dependencies, mismatches, bus\nholds, x0 and strict RVFI ordering. The unchanged run_coremark_ipc\nvalidator passes ten stalled iterations with exactly 4,201,709 bracketed\ncycles, all canonical CRCs, the required UART literal and no out-of-bounds\naccess. This combines to probe fitness 171.41, approximately 10.28 percent\nabove the champion, exceeding the 4.6 percent threshold. This is probe\nevidence; implementation-phase correctness and fitness gates remain\nauthoritative. Other workloads can pay extra recovery cycles for wide\noperands whose predictions nevertheless match. Acceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 65.311) * (4,201,709 / cycles_new) > 1.046; at unchanged cycles\nmedian Fmax must exceed 68.316 MHz.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Add a combinational certificate from original EX/MEM operands: any\nzero operand; for MUL, two sign-extended 16-bit operands; for high\nproducts, two zero-extended 16-bit operands. Replace only the recovery\nmismatch predicate with failure of this certificate, keeping every\nexisting validity, legality, destination, reset and MEM-advancement\nqualifier. Under ALTOPS certify all multiplication operations because\nthe two helpers' formulas coincide. Keep the exact multiplier and\narchitectural writeback, original RVFI data, saved repair target,\nrecovery latency and priority, bus effects and held-WB behavior intact.\nThe real recovery cone must contain no exact-product comparator.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_mul_repair.py','description':'''Add focused MEM recovery checks using the existing exact multiplier\nand an independent full-width integer reference. Exercise all four\noperations, signs and 16-bit boundaries, zero times arbitrary wide\nvalues, deterministic random operands, certified matches, required\nmismatches and intentionally conservative replays of matching products.\nFor every non-repaired legal nonzero writer, assert predicted result\nmatches exact arithmetic. Always assert exact WB product and original\nsource metadata. Verify held transactions, reset, invalid/illegal\npayloads, reg_write=0, x0 and once-only advancement qualification. Test\nALTOPS separately against its unchanged formulas; retain all existing\nmultiplier and ALU arithmetic tests.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain every existing architectural, RVFI, arithmetic, multiply repair\nand bubble-free certified-match check. Add wide operands that force\nrequired and conservative repairs, followed by dependent ALU writers,\nloads, all store widths, branches, JALR and DIV/REM. Check exact retired\nproducts and original operands, strict consecutive orders, one repair\nper advancing instruction, no younger wrong-path memory or divider\noperation, oldest-repair priority, stable dmem requests through stalls,\ncorrect replay PC and finite progress. Explicitly test conservative\nrepair even when the numerical prediction matches, without changing\nany hard-invariant checks or the external cosimulation contract.\n'''}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90,default_flow_style=False))\nprint(p)\nprint('Projected fitness',72.022*1e6*10/4201709)\nprint('Projected delta_pct',(72.022/65.311-1)*100)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
