{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_112", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r8s0.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r8s0',\n'title':'Buffer direction-table updates with exact same-cycle read bypass',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 188.51 at 79.208 MHz with 4,201,709 measured CoreMark cycles. Its 18.733 ns worst path starts at MEM/WB load data and runs through WB forwarding and EX branch resolution into an IF direction-table register. The late branch result controls updates to a 64-entry resettable counter table, creating routing and feedback costs. LESSONS.md is absent; the micro_opt history and recent outcomes were reviewed. Local arithmetic and operand-selection rewrites did not produce an acceptance-level vendor timing gain. Isolating the table's physical update from the late EX outcome is the most promising remaining local optimization.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add a one-entry pending-update record inside if_stage: valid, six-bit counter index and the two-bit already saturated next counter value. Capture an advancing EX training event into that record on exactly the original training edge. On the following edge write the previous pending value into direction_q from registered index/data/enable. Consume that previous record while accepting a new one, allowing one training event per cycle. No event queue or extra stall is needed because the table accepts one update every cycle.\n\nPreserve the original predictor's complete cycle-by-cycle behavior with two exact bypasses. For prediction at pc[7:2], use pending_counter_q[1] when the old pending record is valid and its index matches the fetch index; otherwise use direction_q[pc[7:2]][1]. For constructing a new training successor, use the full pending_counter_q on a pending-index match to branch_train_index; otherwise use direction_q[branch_train_index]. Increment/decrement that effective two-bit counter with the existing saturation rules according to branch_train_taken. The training bypass is essential for consecutive updates to the same entry: never calculate the next value from stale backing-table state. Different-index consecutive events commit the older value while the newer one becomes the record exposed to prediction. With no new event, commit the pending value and clear its valid bit.\n\nReset the complete backing table to 2'b10 and clear pending-valid, with deterministic index/data initialization. Nonblocking assignment semantics preserve pre-edge predictions when fetch acceptance coincides with EX training. A redirected fetch uses the same effective direction state as the champion. Keep EX's existing training qualification unchanged, including advance, alignment, legality, holds and older multiply repair. Do not cancel a previously captured event on a later repair: its training was already visible in the champion on its capture edge. Do not change the 64 entries, PC[7:2] indexing, two-bit policy, initial state, predicted targets, recovery latency or CoreMark instruction scheduling.\n\nThis is a small physical retiming of the existing counter-table write with exact forwarding of the pending state, not a new prediction policy. Preserve every top-level nret=1 port, all branch outcomes and RVFI values, original source forwarding, decoder legality, real and ALTOPS arithmetic, memory requests, divider protocol and registered multiply recovery. The intended benefit is that EX's late branch outcome drives only the tiny pending-counter record; table write fanout and table feedback are driven by registers. Added prediction/training bypass muxes and another EX critical path are the principal timing risks.\n\nExpect eight percent higher Fmax and fitness with exactly 4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Acceptance at unchanged cycles needs median Fmax above 82.852 MHz and fitness above 197.18146. Validate all eight saturating transitions, consecutive same/different-index events, aliases, simultaneous prediction and training, disabled training, pending commit with no replacement, reset with a pending event, frontend holds and recovery. Compare the effective predictor to the unchanged reference state on every cycle. Run unchanged formal, stalled cosimulation, canonical CoreMark validation and all vendor placement options before claiming a gain.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add the nine-bit pending-update record and update the backing direction table only from the previous registered record. Bypass the record's high bit to a matching prediction read and both bits to matching training-state reads. Capture the exact saturated successor on the original EX-training edge, allow simultaneous old commit/new capture, and clear pending validity when no replacement arrives. Preserve weakly-taken table reset, reset priority, pre-edge prediction semantics, existing ports and all fetch targets/acceptance/redirect behavior.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_fetch_predictor.py','description':'''Retain all existing prediction, reset, aliasing and same-edge assertions. Add deterministic mixed training/read sequences against a 64-entry integer saturating reference with all eight transitions; include consecutive same-index and different-index updates, prediction aliases, disabled training and reset coincident with a pending write. Check predictions before and after each edge and ensure an accepted fetch uses the pre-edge prediction.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all existing predictor scoreboard, training qualification/order, forwarding, memory hold, divider, exact arithmetic and recovery assertions. Add tightly spaced branches sharing an index, back-to-back differing-index training and recovery while a pending event exists. Check identical fetch acceptance, next PCs, branch directions and training counts with no wrong-path side effects or lost events.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Pending-update hypothesis written and schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
