{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261005-001-r9s0','hyp-20261005-002-r7s1','hyp-20261005-002-r8s1']:\n  print(d['id'])\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('motivation') if isinstance(h,dict) else d.get('motivation'))\n  if d['id']\"'!='\"'hyp-20261005-002-r8s1':\n   print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('PATH',d.get('critical_path'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-002-r7s1\nThe champion scores 178.53 at 75.013 MHz and 4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its fetch PC advances only when ID/EX captures an instruction; load-use bubbles, data-memory waits and divider waits therefore waste otherwise ready instruction-bus cycles. With approximately 22 percent independent stalls on both buses, buffering complete fetched instructions can overlap those waits. LESSONS.md is absent. The structural history includes successful operand/bypass retiming and registered multiply recovery, while an earlier registered-redirect experiment lost throughput. Isolated probes in this hypothesis-generation session found that moving multiplication to MEM alone and retiming the WB result mux do not exceed the current fitness threshold. The proposed single structural angle is frontend/backend decoupling; the existing execution and prediction datapaths remain the champion's.\nInsert an eight-entry elastic instruction FIFO between the existing fetch predictor/PC and decode. Each entry holds the full instruction, its fetch PC and the exact predicted_taken bit used to advance the fetch PC. Fetch accepts io_imemData and advances the predicted PC whenever io_imemReady is high, the FIFO has capacity, and no redirect is being applied, independently of load-use, dmem and divider holds in the backend. Decode consumes only the oldest queued entry when ID/EX can advance without a flush. Independent fetch and consume events can occur on the same edge. This keeps one instruction issue and one retirement per cycle while buffering bus availability across backend stalls.\n\nImplement an eight-slot circular queue with three-bit head/tail pointers and a four-bit occupancy count. Only occupied entries or a ready empty pass-through instruction are valid; full queues never overwrite live entries. Drive fetch space exclusively from registered occupancy < 8; do not feed dequeue or redirect combinationally into this capacity signal. A full queue may dequeue, but cannot accept a replacement until the following cycle sh\n…[10695 of 13767 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a736c9ed93a5d09b]…\nwith the existing single load-use bubble, divider holding, memory\nbackpressure, multiply repair/quarantine, prediction and retirement\nunchanged. Preserve nret=1 and all external ports and invariants.\n\nThe falsifiable prediction is identical bracketed CoreMark cycles\nand an eight percent increase in median Fmax and fitness, from removing\ntwo serial wide selections after the MEM return value. Acceptance at\nunchanged cycles requires median Fmax above 89.269824 MHz and fitness\nabove 212.71456. Other MEM formatting or EX arithmetic paths and vendor\nfactoring of the explicit one-hot logic may cap the gain. Validate\nwith the existing OC/pipeline tests, unchanged formal and stalled\ncosimulation/CoreMark CRC gates, then all three vendor placement\noptions; an area change or one favorable placement alone is not a win.\n\nPATH {'from': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_mul_predictor/narrow_product_31_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.413, 'arrival_ns': 16.487, 'logic_cells': 9, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_oc']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
