{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('id') in ['hyp-20261005-002-r5s1','hyp-20261005-002-r8s1','hyp-20261005-002-r9s1']:\n  print(r['id'],r['title'])\n  h=r['hypothesis']; print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:', r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-002-r5s1 Retime operand preparation and one-hot bypass decisions into ID\nMake one structural change to the ID/EX operand-delivery boundary:\nprepare PC/immediate operands and resolve the next instruction's\nbypass decisions in ID, then register both data and selections on\nits normal capture edge. Keep the existing pipeline depth, issue\nrate, register-file module and every instruction's architectural\nlatency. Preserve the champion's narrow multiply prediction with\nexact MEM validation, branch prediction and iterative division.\n\nAdd alu_a_val and alu_b_val to id_ex_t. ID captures alu_a_val as\ndec_is_auipc ? fetched_pc : rs1_data, and alu_b_val as dec_alu_src ?\ndecoded_imm : rs2_data, using the existing write-first RF outputs.\nHold and clear these fields with the rest of ID/EX. Retain the\noriginal source values, addresses, PC and controls for architectural\nforwarding, stores, branches, multiplication and RVFI.\n\nChange forward_unit from live EX tag comparison to registered,\nthree-way one-hot selections aligned with ID/EX. For each currently\nfetched source address, compare first against the current ID/EX\ndestination that will enter EX/MEM on this edge, then against the\ncurrent EX/MEM destination that will enter MEM/WB. Select bits are\n001 for captured ID data, 010 for next EX/MEM data, and 100 for next\nMEM/WB data. A matching nonzero near writer wins over a far writer;\nx0 never forwards. Export qualified write indications from EX's\npost-trap control and MEM's post-alignment-trap control, each gated\nby its instruction validity. Do not qualify from raw decoder write\nbits: a misaligned JAL/JALR or load must not become a bypass writer.\nSample selections only when ID/EX captures, retain them when ID/EX\nstalls, and reset/flush them to 001 whenever ID/EX resets/flushes.\nSelection flops may live in forward_unit with exactly the same\nclock/reset/stall/flush conditions as ID, avoiding extra bundle fields.\n\nIn EX, replace each architectural rs1/rs2 priority mux with the OR\nof three masked candidates: original ID value\n…[12583 of 15655 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9c9260836a1cacf]…\n\nretirement, decoder/trap rules, memory alignment/side-effect checks,\nRVFI source values and strictly consecutive retirement order. No\nevaluation-contract path changes are proposed.\n\nValidate every source combination, saved-source priority, same-rd\nproducer shadowing, x0, architectural versus ALU roles, captured source\nretention and both recovery causes. Retain existing full-rate ALU and\nsingle-bubble load-use timing assertions. Run unchanged formal,\nready/stalled trace cosim, canonical CoreMark CRC/validation literal\nand MMIO brackets, and all three vendor Gowin placement options.\nAcceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 85.344) * (4,196,601 / cycles_new) > 1.046;\nwith identical scheduling the median Fmax must exceed 89.270 MHz.\nThe expected gain is an estimate. Main risks are synthesis refactoring\nthe crossbar back into serial muxes, mask fanout, and another EX control\npath becoming dominant. Preliminary measurements will be recorded below;\nthey do not substitute for implementation-phase correctness gates.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
