{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20261005-002-r3s1','hyp-20261005-002-r8s1','hyp-20261005-001-r9s0'):\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n  print('notes:',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261005-002-r3s1 Move multiplication into a registered shared execution unit\nStarting from the supplied champion, remove MUL, MULH, MULHU and MULHSU\nfrom the single-cycle ALU result cone and execute them in one shared,\nregistered multiplier transaction. Keep ordinary integer operations,\nprediction, control-flow recovery, memory behavior and the existing\niterative divider unchanged. Capture the fully forwarded 32-bit source\noperands, operation and instruction payload once on a valid unstalled\nlaunch. Feed one unsigned 32-by-32 multiplication exclusively from\nthese operand registers and capture its full 64-bit product in another\nregister. MUL and MULHU finish immediately when this product register\nis written; select the appropriate registered half directly, without\ntaking high-word correction states or adding another result-copy cycle.\nFor MULH and MULHSU, in subsequent registered steps correct the high word using\nhigh_signed = high_unsigned - (signed_a && a[31] ? b : 0)\n                            - (signed_b && b[31] ? a : 0), modulo 2^32.\nsigned_a applies to MULH and MULHSU; signed_b applies only to MULH.\nSeparate the two 32-bit correction subtractions with a register so they\ncannot recreate a long multiply-plus-correction combinational path.\nMULHSU needs only the first correction; MULH needs both. A nominal MUL\nlaunched on edge N captures its product and asserts result-valid on\nN+1, then is consumed into EX/MEM with frontend release on N+2. Thus\nthe measured workload pays two extra issue cycles per multiply, rather\nthan the latency of signed correction for every low-word operation.\nEliminate the original three combinational product expressions from\nalu.out; merely registering a selected output after that cone is not\nsufficient. This is structural isolation of multiplication latency,\nnot a change to RV32M arithmetic or the retirement width.\n\nProvide start/busy/result-valid/consume control, analogous to the current\ndivider. Hold fetch and ID/EX during a multiply, let older MEM/WB work\ndrain, and write side\n…[15040 of 18112 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a58407a138347437]…\nents and\n  stalled bracketed CoreMark completed.\n\nMeasured placement Fmax values: 87.616, 91.186 and 91.186 MHz.\nMedian Fmax: 91.186 MHz. Fitness: 217.29 iterations/second.\nCoreMark: exactly 4,196,601 bracketed cycles for ten iterations, unchanged\nfrom the champion. Both required thresholds are exceeded: 89.269824 MHz\nand 212.71456 fitness. The measured improvement is about 6.85%, below the\nfull eight-percent prediction.\n\nArea: 4,222 logic cells versus 4,025 previously (+197); 1,329 registers\nunchanged; eight LUT-RAM, six BSRAM and six DSP. Option 0's worst setup path\nis now 16.487 ns across nine cells from data RAM through MEM/OC into the\nmultiplier input register absorbed by synthesis, versus the reported\n17.775 ns ten-cell baseline path. Other EX arithmetic/control and OC\nretention paths remain close behind it. Placement option 0 alone misses\nthe acceptance threshold; the measured median across all three options\npasses. The area increase and remaining critical paths are the main\nimplementation tradeoffs.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
