{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nids=['hyp-20261005-002-r7s1','hyp-20261005-002-r10s1','hyp-20261005-001-r11s0','hyp-20261005-001-r13s0','hyp-20261005-002-r8s1']\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  print(d['id'],d.get('fitness'),d.get('cycles'),d.get('title'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-002-r7s1 178.84 3975958 Decouple fetch with an eight-entry queue and registered capacity\nInsert an eight-entry elastic instruction FIFO between the existing fetch predictor/PC and decode. Each entry holds the full instruction, its fetch PC and the exact predicted_taken bit used to advance the fetch PC. Fetch accepts io_imemData and advances the predicted PC whenever io_imemReady is high, the FIFO has capacity, and no redirect is being applied, independently of load-use, dmem and divider holds in the backend. Decode consumes only the oldest queued entry when ID/EX can advance without a flush. Independent fetch and consume events can occur on the same edge. This keeps one instruction issue and one retirement per cycle while buffering bus availability across backend stalls.\n\nImplement an eight-slot circular queue with three-bit head/tail pointers and a four-bit occupancy count. Only occupied entries or a ready empty pass-through instruction are valid; full queues never overwrite live entries. Drive fetch space exclusively from registered occupancy < 8; do not feed dequeue or redirect combinationally into this capacity signal. A full queue may dequeue, but cannot accept a replacement until the following cycle shows space. When occupancy is between one and seven, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue advances both pointers and leaves count unchanged. This intentionally breaks the backend-to-fetch ready path at registered occupancy. Reset and redirect clear count and pointers with priority over enqueue/dequeue. Payload bits need not reset, but stored payload cannot produce a valid instruction after the clearing edge; no unknown bits may reach a valid retirement. Use an empty-queue pass-through: when count is zero, present the current fetch bundle to decode with validity qualified by io_imemReady. A ready instruction can be enqueued and dequeued on the same edge without becoming a live buffered entry; if decode cannot accept it, enqueue it and retain it at the head. For a nonempty queue, decode always sees the oldes\n…[23206 of 26278 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2bff2daca78cadd1]…\nivider transactions,\nbus handshakes, memory alignment checks, sole nret=1 retirement boundary\nand consecutive RVFI order. Flush and older repair still discard all\nyounger payload and saved-source state, and downstream holds still apply.\n\nThe estimated six-percent fitness gain has low confidence. Fewer false\nload dependencies can improve iterations/cycle, while inactive unused\nroutes reduce forwarding-control activity and fanout. Source-use gating\ncan itself increase timing depth, and the true-dependency rate may leave\nlittle cycle headroom. Measure both factors instead of assuming a win:\nacceptance requires (Fmax_new/91.186)*(4196601/cycles_new) > 1.046.\nRun unchanged formal, unit and field-by-field ready/stalled cosim, the\ncanonical 2K CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket/bounded-access checks and all\nthree Gowin placements. Preserve existing assertions on real sources\nand exact raw RVFI fields; adapt only artificial stage fixtures that\ndeliberately treat immediate or PC fields as execution dependencies.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
