{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwanted={'hyp-20261005-002-r7s1','hyp-20261005-002-r9s1','hyp-20261005-002-r12s1','hyp-20261005-002-r13s1','hyp-20261005-002-r11s1'}\nfor s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(s)\n if r['id'] in wanted:\n  print('\\\\nID',r['id'],'\\\\nTITLE',r['title'])\n  h=r['hypothesis']; print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwanted={'hyp-20261005-002-r7s1','hyp-20261005-002-r9s1','hyp-20261005-002-r12s1','hyp-20261005-002-r13s1','hyp-20261005-002-r11s1'}\nfor s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(s)\n if r['id'] in wanted:\n  print('\\\\nID',r['id'],'\\\\nTITLE',r['title'])\n  h=r['hypothesis']; print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261005-002-r7s1 \nTITLE Decouple fetch with an eight-entry queue and registered capacity\nInsert an eight-entry elastic instruction FIFO between the existing fetch predictor/PC and decode. Each entry holds the full instruction, its fetch PC and the exact predicted_taken bit used to advance the fetch PC. Fetch accepts io_imemData and advances the predicted PC whenever io_imemReady is high, the FIFO has capacity, and no redirect is being applied, independently of load-use, dmem and divider holds in the backend. Decode consumes only the oldest queued entry when ID/EX can advance without a flush. Independent fetch and consume events can occur on the same edge. This keeps one instruction issue and one retirement per cycle while buffering bus availability across backend stalls.\n\nImplement an eight-slot circular queue with three-bit head/tail pointers and a four-bit occupancy count. Only occupied entries or a ready empty pass-through instruction are valid; full queues never overwrite live entries. Drive fetch space exclusively from registered occupancy < 8; do not feed dequeue or redirect combinationally into this capacity signal. A full queue may dequeue, but cannot accept a replacement until the following cycle shows space. When occupancy is between one and seven, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue advances both pointers and leaves count unchanged. This intentionally breaks the backend-to-fetch ready path at registered occupancy. Reset and redirect clear count and pointers with priority over enqueue/dequeue. Payload bits need not reset, but stored payload cannot produce a valid instruction after the clearing edge; no unknown bits may reach a valid retirement. Use an empty-queue pass-through: when count is zero, present the current fetch bundle to decode with validity qualified by io_imemReady. A ready instruction can be enqueued and dequeued on the same edge without becoming a live buffered entry; if decode cannot accept it, enqueue it and retain it at the head. For a nonempty queue, decode always sees the oldest st\n…[24597 of 27669 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:acdf3a620887cd9e]…\napper and timing contracts remain intact.\n\nPrior profiling counted 93,960 MULs and no DIV/REM in the measured run.\nOne extra issue cycle per low-word MUL is about 2.24 percent of the\nchampion's cycle count before overlap with existing stalls. With that\nconservative estimate, acceptance requires median Fmax above about\n97.52 MHz, and a ten-percent fitness gain requires about 102.55 MHz.\nRemoving both speculative return arithmetic and exact MEM validation\nshould reduce DSP placement pressure and repair-control fanout, but\nlive MEM loads and ordinary EX arithmetic can remain limiting. The gain\nis unmeasured. Require (Fmax_new / 91.186) * (4196601 / cycles_new) > 1.046.\nValidate exact random and directed multiplication, nearest-writer\npriority, completion during bus holds, consecutive MULs, MUL/DIV\ntransitions and recovery cancellation. Run unchanged formal, unit\nsuites, ready/stalled trace cosim, canonical 2K CoreMark CRCs and UART\nvalidation, MMIO timing brackets and all three vendor Gowin placements.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
