{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_66", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat fpga/core_bench_si.sv'", "aggregated_output": "// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the rationale on LFSR-driven imem, the dmem\n// model, the V2 stall generator, and why the LED observes only the\n// memory-side outputs (RVFI-only logic is pruned).\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n    if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n    if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n    if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n  end\n  assign dmem_rdata = dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]];\n\n  logic [31:0] imem_addr;\n  // Single-channel RVFI port set (NRET=1 contract). Connected for\n  // elaboration only; nothing observes it, so synthesis prunes it.\n  logic        rvfi_valid_0;\n  logic [63:0] rvfi_order_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_rd_wdata_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_mem_addr_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0;\n  logic [4:0]  rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0;\n  logic [3:0]  rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0;\n  logic [1:0]  rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0;\n  logic        rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0;\n\n  // V2: memory-ready signals come from the same stall sequence the\n  // CoreMark simulation uses, so stall-handling logic is part of the\n  // timed netlist (fpga/bench_stall_gen.sv).\n  logic imem_ready, dmem_ready;\n  bench_stall_gen stall_gen (\n    .clock      (clock),\n    .reset      (reset),\n    .imem_ready (imem_ready),\n    .dmem_ready (dmem_ready)\n  );\n\n  core cpu (\n    .clock            (clock),\n    .reset            (reset),\n    .io_imemAddr      (imem_addr),\n    .io_imemData      (lfsr),\n    .io_imemReady     (imem_ready),\n    .io_dmemAddr      (dmem_addr),\n    .io_dmemWData     (dmem_wdata),\n    .io_dmemRData     (dmem_rdata),\n    .io_dmemWEn       (dmem_wen),\n    .io_dmemREn       (dmem_ren),\n    .io_dmemReady     (dmem_ready),\n    .io_rvfi_valid_0    (rvfi_valid_0),\n    .io_rvfi_order_0    (rvfi_order_0),\n    .io_rvfi_insn_0     (rvfi_insn_0),\n    .io_rvfi_trap_0     (rvfi_trap_0),\n    .io_rvfi_halt_0     (rvfi_halt_0),\n    .io_rvfi_intr_0     (rvfi_intr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mode_0     (rvfi_mode_0),\n    .io_rvfi_ixl_0      (rvfi_ixl_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs1_addr_0 (rvfi_rs1_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs1_rdata_0(rvfi_rs1_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs2_addr_0 (rvfi_rs2_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rs2_rdata_0(rvfi_rs2_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rd_addr_0  (rvfi_rd_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_rd_wdata_0 (rvfi_rd_wdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_pc_rdata_0 (rvfi_pc_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_pc_wdata_0 (rvfi_pc_wdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_addr_0 (rvfi_mem_addr_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_rmask_0(rvfi_mem_rmask_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_wmask_0(rvfi_mem_wmask_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_rdata_0(rvfi_mem_rdata_0),\n    .io_rvfi_mem_wdata_0(rvfi_mem_wdata_0)\n  );\n\n  // V2: observe only the memory-side outputs. Everything the core computes\n  // reaches the fetch address or a store, so the datapath stays in the\n  // netlist; logic that only drives RVFI (a verification port a deployed\n  // core would not have) is pruned and does not count toward Fmax or area.\n  assign led = ^{imem_addr, dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, dmem_rdata};\n\nendmodule\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"agent_message","text":"The register-file prototype still misses the acceptance threshold. I’m refining that hypothesis around a specific remaining path: fold the registered write-collision and reset-validity handling into OC’s existing capture masks, removing the mux before them. This refinement is unmeasured, so the proposal will retain low confidence."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_68", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json\nfrom jsonschema import validate\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r15s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-002-r15s1',\n'title':'Retimed synchronous register-file banks with collision bypass folded into OC',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 217.29 at 91.186 MHz and 4,196,601 measured\nCoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. The structural history was\nreviewed: r8s1 improved fitness by separating operand collection from\nexecution, while r10s1's extra load-return stage and r14s1's post-OC\nload-bank mux regressed. r9s0 improved the champion by folding operand\nselection into parallel capture masks. r5s0's flop register file also\nregressed. These results favor using existing register boundaries and\nflattened capture selection rather than adding execution-input muxes.\n\nIsolated fetch-buffer probes passed ready/stalled traces and canonical\nCoreMark checks but lost too much Gowin frequency. A synchronous\nregister-file prototype preserved exactly 4,196,601 CoreMark cycles and\npassed all eight selftest/random-program ready/stalled trace comparisons.\nHowever, its registered write-first/validity output mux precedes the\nexisting OC capture crossbar. A padded-memory variant measured only\n83.546 MHz median, so that prototype is a regression and is not the\nproposed final circuit. The promising remaining structural refinement is\nto move those snapshot selections into OC's existing scalar route masks.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the asynchronous register file plus separate ID operand\nsnapshot flops with two replicated synchronous read banks, using the\nexisting ID-to-OC capture edge as the memory read boundary. Broadcast\nthe sole WB write to both banks. Read the incoming decoded instruction's\nrs1 and rs2 addresses on exactly the edge that captures its ID/OC\nmetadata. Hold the registered bank outputs whenever ID/OC is held.\nThere is no additional instruction stage, dependency bubble, load-return\nstage or mux after OC/EX. Keep all EX, MEM, WB and divider behavior at\nthe champion's boundaries.\n\nExpose the raw registered bank words directly to OC. Also capture and\nhold, on that same decode edge, each source's nonzero/initialized bit,\neach source's WB write-collision bit, and one shared 32-bit WB write\nvalue. Use a resettable 32-bit initialized plane instead of resetting\nRAM contents: reset clears logical register validity, accepted nonzero\nwrites set the corresponding bit, and both replicas receive identical\nwrites. Capture the pre-edge initialized state and explicitly handle\nsame-edge WB writes. A same-edge matching nonzero write selects the\ncaptured WB value even when that register was previously uninitialized.\nAn uninitialized noncolliding register and x0 select zero. Raw RAM data\nmay be unknown while invalid, but must never appear in a valid operand.\nDo not rely on a device-specific read-during-write result or RAM power-up\ncontents. Reset and recovery discard snapshot ownership through the\nexisting instruction-valid and saved-source rules.\n\nCrucially, do not reconstruct a write-first architectural snapshot with\na 32-bit collision/zero mux between the RAM output and operand_stage.\nFold this selection into the existing mutually exclusive OC capture\nmasks. Preserve saved-source priority and qualified EX, MEM, WB nearest\nproducer priority. Only when the existing forwarding route selects the\nID snapshot may its data split into two parallel terms: raw registered\nbank data masked by nonzero, initialized and \"'!collision; or the shared\ncaptured WB value masked by nonzero and collision. If neither term is\neligible, the source is zero. Live EX/MEM/WB routes must suppress both\nID terms. A matching unavailable nearest producer must still block\ncapture instead of falling back to any snapshot. Saved sources remain\nauthoritative after their producer or collision snapshot drains.\n\nApply the same flattened masks directly to all four current capture\nroles: architectural rs1, architectural rs2, prepared ALU a and prepared\nALU b. PC and immediate remain parallel terms using existing role masks.\nThe ordinary resolved-source network used for independent retention\nmust also consume the raw/collision candidates directly. Never feed a\ncompleted snapshot mux into a second operand mux. Every role still ends\nat the existing OC/EX flops; EX arithmetic receives the same already\nresolved operands as the champion. Preserve full-rate dependent ordinary\nALU issue and the current one-bubble immediate load-use schedule.\n\nPair raw bank outputs and all snapshot metadata with the exact ID/OC\ninstruction across advances and holds. The bank read enable, snapshot\nflags and captured collision value share the existing ID/OC acceptance\ncondition, including captures into an empty decoded slot during an older\nEX hold. Do not infer an extra RAM output-register stage. Ignore bank\nreads on discarded recovery-edge payloads, and ensure the first target\ninstruction obtains a fresh matched read on its own decode edge. Eliminate\nthe now redundant ID rs-value data registers and unused ID-prepared\noperand copies from the timed datapath. Keep original architectural\nsource addresses and exact resolved source values for RVFI.\n\nUse ordinary synthesizable synchronous memory semantics in simulation\nand formal. Map the replicated banks to native Gowin block memory if\npossible, with an appropriate declaration/attribute and physical depth\npadding when needed. Verify actual inference in the vendor report;\ndeclaring an attribute alone is insufficient. Any device-specific\nimplementation must have an equivalent unrestricted formal/simulation\nmodel, with the same enabled-read latency, holds, writes, logical reset\nand explicit collision bypass. Never assume away read collisions or\nuninitialized addresses. Do not modify synthesis scripts or wrappers.\n\nThis is one structural retiming of register-file storage, write-first\nsnapshot handling and operand capture. The expected eight-percent gain\nhas low confidence and is not measured. The earlier straightforward\nsynchronous prototype regressed; the final flattened raw-bank/collision\ncapture has not been built or timed. Its intended benefit is removal of\nthe additional snapshot mux while reducing asynchronous read routing and\nresettable operand storage. Remaining live MEM-load and EX-result return\npaths can still dominate, and extra mask fanout or memory placement can\nnegate the gain. Acceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 91.186) * (4196601 / cycles_new) > 1.046;\nwith unchanged cycles, median Gowin Fmax must exceed 95.381 MHz.\n\nPreserve nret=1, all top-level ports, sole MEM/WB retirement, consecutive\nRVFI order, decoder default-illegal and SYSTEM policy, alignment traps,\nbranch prediction and recovery, older multiply repair/quarantine, divider\ncancellation, exact real and ALTOPS arithmetic, all bus handshakes and\nevery hard invariant. Run unchanged formal including progress and cover,\nexact operand/retention and register-file tests, ready/stalled trace\ncosims, canonical 2K CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket/bounded-access checks\nand all three vendor placements. Prototype results do not replace these\nimplementation-phase gates.\n'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Implement replicated synchronous raw read banks with a shared WB\nwrite and resettable initialized plane. Add an accepted-read enable;\nhold raw outputs, per-source initialized/nonzero/collision snapshots\nand one shared WB-value snapshot together. Export raw data and scalar\nsnapshot metadata without a wide output write-first/zero mux. Preserve\nlogical zero reset, x0 and exact same-edge write-first semantics; verify\nnative block-memory inference and exactly one read edge.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Capture decoded metadata on the existing edge and pair it with the\nregister file's synchronous raw snapshots. Remove redundant clocked\narchitectural source copies and unused prepared operand data, avoiding\nan extra output register. Expose the snapshot sidecar consistently\nthrough ID/OC holds and recovery; preserve legality and PC/prediction\nmetadata. All architectural source resolution remains in OC.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Represent the ID/OC raw-source and collision snapshot sidecar as\nneeded for exact instruction ownership. Keep initialization/collision\nflags and the shared captured WB value paired with raw reads through\nholds. Do not add bank references or late source muxes to EX; OC/EX\ncontinues to carry fully resolved architectural and ALU operands.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/operand_stage.sv','description':'''Split only the ID-snapshot route into raw-bank and captured-WB terms\ninside each existing flattened candidate OR. Fold initialized, nonzero\nand collision bits into scalar enables for architectural, retained and\nprepared-ALU roles. Preserve nearest-writer readiness, saved-source\npriority, flush/hold/advance behavior and all live producer bypasses.\nRemove any preceding snapshot data mux rather than adding another\nwide selection level or moving selection into EX.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire accepted synchronous RF reads and the raw/flag/collision-value\nsidecar between RF, ID and OC with the existing decoded-slot acceptance\nand hold rules. Preserve exact operand/metadata pairing, every external\nport, branch/multiply recovery priority, divider transactions and sole\nretirement/order logic. Keep all current MEM-load and EX-result bypasses.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Adapt tests to the synchronous raw-snapshot interface while retaining\nlogical reset, x0, register contents and write-first checks. Verify\nboth replicas, enabled/held reads, uninitialized masking, same-edge\nwrites to initialized and cold registers, collisions on both ports,\nreset after writes, and changes on unrelated WB cycles during a hold.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_operand_stage.py','description':'''Extend packed layouts and candidates for the raw RF and collision\nsidecar. Check every live/saved/ID route, initialized and x0 masks,\ncollision versus live-producer priority, unavailable nearest writers,\nindependent saved-source retention, and all four architectural/ALU\ncapture roles. Preserve full-rate issue and existing exact assertions.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_ex_divider.py','description':'''Update shared bundle packing declarations only as required by the\nnew ID/OC sidecar. Retain all exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, transaction,\nheld payload, cancellation and consumption checks; completed OC/EX\noperands must retain their original semantics and layout coverage.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Update internal snapshot ownership observations while preserving\narchitectural assertions. Cover WB collisions at decode, held snapshots\nwhile WB changes, two distinct source writers, load/ALU/MUL/DIV/store/\nbranch/JALR consumers, reset, both recoveries and independent bus stalls.\nRequire exact RVFI operands and unchanged ordinary dependency timing,\nwith no added instruction stage, drop, duplicate or wrong-path effect.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=80))\nvalidate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261005-002-r15s1' and h['category']=='structural'\nprint('Final hypothesis schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
