           wt / \"cores\" / a.target / \"generated\" / \"timing\", options=opts)\ntools/eval/gowin.py:221:        tr = (wt / \"cores\" / a.target / \"generated\" / \"timing\" / f\"gowin_p{r['place_option']}\" / \"impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\")\nfpga/bench_stall_gen.sv:13:// That logic then saved simulated cycles at no area or timing cost. With\nfpga/constraints/clock.sdc:1:// Gowin timing constraint for the V2 FPGA fitness (tools/eval/gowin.py).\nfpga/constraints/clock.sdc:2:// The target only steers timing-driven optimization; the score is the\nfpga/constraints/clock.sdc:3:// Actual Fmax from the timing report. Fixed for every design.\nfpga/core_bench.sv:13://      forwarding muxes all stay in the netlist for timing.\nfpga/scripts/nextpnr_run.sh:26:  --timing-allow-fail \\\ncores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv:3:// Operand forwarding. For each rs in the EX-stage's source list, picks\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv:11:// Order matters: this file MUST be the first source passed to any tool\ncores/bench/rtl/mul_exact.sv:1:// MEM validation uses the original registered, full-width source operands.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n print(r['id'], r.get('category'), r.get('outcome'), 'fitness',r.get('fitness'),'fmax',r.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',r.get('cycles'))\n if r.get('category') == 'structural':\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('TITLE:',r['title'])\n  print('MOTIVATION:',r.get('motivation'))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n print(r['id'], r.get('category'), r.get('outcome'), 'fitness',r.get('fitness'),'fmax',r.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',r.get('cycles'))\n if r.get('category') == 'structural':\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('TITLE:',r['title'])\n  print('MOTIVATION:',r.get('motivation'))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-df5bec5 micro_opt improvement fitness 12.12 fmax 5.442 cycles 4491485\nhyp-20261005-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression fitness 13.11 fmax 5.888 cycles 4491485\nhyp-20261005-002-r1s1 structural improvement fitness 104.51 fmax 46.944 cycles 4491817\nTITLE: Remove the combinational divide chain with a shared iterative divider\nMOTIVATION: The only recorded experiment is the baseline: fitness 12.12, median Fmax\n5.442 MHz, and a 199.837 ns worst path containing 156 cells through\nforwarding and u_ex/u_alu. There are no prior structural attempts or\nrecorded lessons for this core. The four combinational division/remainder\noperators in alu.sv are the strongest candidate for this exceptionally\ndeep arithmetic cone. Even infrequent DIV/REM instructions place that\nhardware on the shared execute-result path and constrain the clock used\nby every instruction. Serializing division should substantially raise\nFmax and reduce LUT pressure, provided its added CoreMark cycles cost\nless than the frequency improvement under the existing bus stalls.\n\nHYPOTHESIS: Replace only the real-hardware DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one\nclocked radix-2 restoring divider, shared across all four operations.\nCapture the fully forwarded operands and operation exactly once when a\nvalid division instruction can start. Use registered divisor, quotient,\nand 33-bit partial remainder; execute one trial subtraction and quotient\nbit per cycle for 32 iterations. Register signed-magnitude preparation\nand final sign correction separately so neither forms a long chain with\nthe iterative subtractor. Quotient sign is the operand-sign XOR, while\nremainder sign follows the dividend. Handle zero divisor and signed\nINT_MIN/-1 explicitly with the existing RV32M bit results. Eliminate all\nsynthesizable division and modulo operators from the real ALU branch.\nKeep base integer operations and the existing multiplier implementation\non their current single-cycle path; this is one change in the structure\nof division execution, with no predictor or memory redesi\n…[11811 of 14883 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:324e8d17bb324c6b]…\nan unmeasured\nestimate. Two extra cycles for each of 93,960 MULs add at most 187,920\ncycles, or 4.47 percent, before overlap with existing bus stalls. Under\nthat conservative cycle estimate, the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold\nrequires median Fmax above 54.064 MHz; a 20 percent fitness gain requires\nabout 62.024 MHz. Isolating DSP multiplication from the long operand and\nresult mux paths, and reducing three products to one, makes that a\nplausible target, while branch recovery or another integer path can\nbecome the next limit. Validate exact arithmetic, stall behavior,\nunchanged formal and CoreMark\ncosimulation/CRC gates, and median Gowin timing. Acceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 49.474) * (4201709 / cycles_new) > 1.046; at unchanged\ncycles that means Fmax above 51.750 MHz. Preserve the current prediction\nscheme and ordinary instruction latency; the additional latency belongs\nonly to the bounded multiply transaction.\n\nNOTES: \nhyp-20261005-003-r3s2 predictor improvement fitness 138.64 fmax 58.252 cycles 4201709\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
