pu/u_ex\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\",\n      \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/div_payload_q.pc_next_21_s0\",\n      \"slack_ns\": -14.333,\n      \"arrival_ns\": 24.628,\n      \"logic_cells\": 17,\n      \"modules\": [\n        \"clock_ibuf\",\n        \"cpu/u_id\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex\",\n        \"cpu\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_68", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r4s1.yaml')\nv={\n 'id':'hyp-20261005-002-r4s1',\n 'title':'Register multiply repair and quarantine younger memory effects',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 138.64 at median Fmax 58.252 MHz with 4,201,709\ncounted CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. The structural history\nshows that interlocking every multiply added 187,413 counted cycles and\nfailed to beat the champion despite reaching 58.572 MHz. Registering all\ncontrol-flow redirects also lost throughput. The current design avoids\nthose common-case delays with narrow EX products and exact MEM products,\nbut its full-width product comparison directly drives frontend flush,\nEX squash, divider launch suppression and pipeline enables. Read-only\nvendor timing experiments that isolated operand or address paths exposed\nthis remaining loop: EX/MEM opcode through full-width multiplication,\nvalidation and pipeline control, with about 23.6 ns arrival. Registering\nmultiply repair specifically can break this control feedback without\nadding a cycle to correctly predicted multiplies or ordinary branches.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Move only multiplication validation recovery from a combinational MEM\nsignal to a registered MEM/WB repair record. Keep the existing signed\n16-by-16 EX prediction, all full-width exact product variants in MEM,\nordinary EX branch recovery, bimodal prediction, and every instruction's\nnormal data and retirement latency. On each advancing legal multiply\nthat writes a nonzero destination, capture the exact-versus-predicted\nmismatch and its already registered pc_next alongside the true product\nentering MEM/WB. Recovery in the following cycle uses only this repair\nrecord; there must be no full-product/comparison-to-frontend path.\n\nA delayed repair must quarantine the additional younger instruction\nthat has already reached EX/MEM. While registered repair is asserted,\ngate both dmemREn and every dmemWEn lane off before any bus transaction,\nreplace that younger MEM/WB capture with a completely cleared bubble,\nand suppress its generation of another repair. Simultaneously squash\nyounger EX, clear ID/EX, invalidate fetch and install the recorded\nmultiplication pc_next. The original multiply is already in MEM/WB and\nmust retire once with its exact result and original operands. Preserve\nits regfile write and RVFI order. Gate the hazard unit's memory-operation\nqualification by the MEM quarantine signal, so a squashed younger load\nor store and io_dmemReady=0 cannot hold up recovery. Actual surviving\nmemory transactions retain the established stable-request semantics.\nRegistered multiply repair has priority over every younger redirect,\nload-use hazard and tentative divider wait, and fires exactly once.\n\nA younger DIV/REM must not start on the cycle an older multiplication\nis still undergoing unregistered MEM validation: a later repair could\notherwise leave a wrong-path divider busy. Export a metadata-only\nvalidation-pending indication from EX/MEM for valid legal multiply\nwriters to nonzero rd and defer divider launch until that indication\nhas passed. The existing divider wait can retain the younger instruction\nand drain MEM. Snapshot both fully forwarded operands when launch is\ndeferred, including values from an older WB writer that will disappear\non the next edge. If validation passes, launch with those captured\noperands and retain the original source values for RVFI; if registered\nrepair fires, discard the deferred instruction and snapshots without\nstarting or resetting a surviving divider transaction. Do not gate a\nstarted divider's arithmetic with this indication. This rare defer is\nsupporting machinery for the new recovery boundary, not a redesign of\nmultiply or divide arithmetic.\n\nClear repair and deferred-launch state on reset. An older multiplication\nmust not generate a repair while held or be retired again; a squashed\nMEM multiplication must not create a second repair. Correct predictions\nadvance continuously with no quarantine, new interlock, or added cycle.\nThe existing CoreMark profile found 93,960 predicted MUL matches and zero\nDIV/REM instructions, so the proposed recovery latency should leave the\nmeasured 4,201,709-cycle bracket unchanged. Wide-operand mismatches on\nother programs pay one additional recovery cycle, while every RV32IM\nproduct still commits the exact 32-bit result. Preserve the exact ALTOPS\nformulas in both predictor and validator and all real arithmetic.\n\nThis is one structural retiming of multiplication recovery, with no\nregister-file, address-generation, prediction-policy or global pipeline\nchange. Keep nret=1, all top-level ports, illegal-default decoding, traps,\nmemory alignment checks, original RVFI operands and consecutive orders.\nExpect 10 percent higher fitness, with medium confidence; this remains\na timing hypothesis until median placement results are available. Another\nordinary ALU path may become limiting. Require unchanged unit/formal gates,\nreal mismatch tests, stalled CoreMark with canonical CRCs and validation\nliteral, and vendor timing. Acceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 58.252) * (4201709 / cycles_new) > 1.046; at unchanged cycles\nmedian Fmax must exceed 60.932 MHz.\n''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Register mismatch and pc_next when a valid multiplication advances\nwith its exact result into MEM/WB. Drive repair exclusively from that\nrecord. On delayed repair, suppress all younger MEM bus requests,\nclear its would-be MEM/WB payload, and suppress replacement repair\ncreation, while the older exact multiplication retires normally.\nKeep full-width products, alignment and normal stall behavior intact.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Keep older multiply repair ahead of younger EX recovery. Qualify the\nhazard memory-op input so a quarantined MEM instruction cannot freeze\nrecovery. Wire a metadata-only older-multiply validation-pending signal\nto EX. Preserve memory ports, nret=1, ordinary forwarding and RVFI order.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Consume registered older repair as squash, retaining its existing\nsuppression of younger EX/MEM capture, redirects, training and divider\nwait. Defer a new DIV/REM launch during older MEM multiplication\nvalidation, snapshot forwarded operands across this defer, use those\nsnapshots if validation passes, and discard them on repair. Keep normal\nprediction, ALU/divider arithmetic and completion handling unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain existing coverage and add forced multiply mismatches followed\nby loads, stores, branches, JALR, DIV/REM and consecutive multiplies.\nExercise ready and stalled buses on the delayed repair cycle, x0\nmultiply destinations, correct predictions, and older WB forwarding\nto a deferred divide. Assert no wrong-path memory request or retirement,\nexact original RVFI operands, consecutive orders, one-shot recovery,\nfinite progress and unchanged bubble-free prediction matches.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_ex_divider.py','description':'''Drive the added validation-pending input in all existing execute/divider\ntests. Add deferred-launch operand preservation, validation-pass launch,\nrepair-before-launch cancellation and bus stalls during defer, retaining\nall existing exact arithmetic, completion and result-hold checks.\n'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(v,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(v,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
