the\nexisting ALU and divider launch with the newly selected ALU operands;\npreserve the divider's once-only operand/payload capture, completion\nand consume protocol. Retained WB data must remain available during\ndmem stalls even when WB validity clears. Keep the register-file module,\nforwarding priority, hazard controls, prediction, EX/MEM result mux,\npipeline latency, retirement width and all real/ALTOPS arithmetic.\nThis is one local operand-preselection optimization, with no predictor,\ncache, extra execution stage, or additional instruction capability.\n\nAn isolated implementation of exactly this\n…[7991 of 11063 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c883b39dc798b8ca]…\neculative-product forwarding and exact MEM validation.\nPreserve the existing rs2 store-data path, load writeback, real and\nALTOPS arithmetic, instruction legality, all trap suppression, divider\ntransactions, multiplication repair priority and nret=1 ports. Capture,\nhold, flush and squash the address field with its exact EX/MEM payload;\nit must remain stable throughout data-memory stalls. Inactive bus\naddresses are unconstrained, but active requests and all architectural\nvalues must remain identical for arbitrary instruction streams.\n\nExpect unchanged counted CoreMark cycles and an approximately ten\npercent Fmax/fitness increase. At unchanged cycles the acceptance\nthreshold is median Fmax above 60.932 MHz, or fitness above 145.017.\nConfirm with unchanged formal and stalled cosimulation gates and all\ncanonical CoreMark CRCs. Inspect Gowin paths to determine whether\naddress specialization removes the general ALU result selection from\nmemory addressing; other forwarding or control paths may limit gains.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
