{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r5s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Replace inferred register-file RAM with banked flip-flop storage\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 138.64 at 58.252 MHz with 4,201,709 measured\n  CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts at u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\n  and crosses ID, EX operand selection and the ordinary ALU before\n  reaching the timed data-memory interface. Prior structural changes\n  to redirect and multiply-repair registers did not remove this limit;\n  serial multiplication raised Fmax but lost throughput. No prior\n  structural attempt changed register-file storage. Gowin can absorb\n  the existing ID operand registers into inferred synchronous RAM,\n  placing RAM clock-to-output delay ahead of forwarding and arithmetic.\n  Exchanging a small 32-by-32 RAM for explicit banked flip-flops offers\n  a way to shorten that path without extra instruction latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the integer register file's inferred array with an\n  explicit flip-flop implementation, arranged as four banks of eight\n  architectural register numbers. Implement x1 through x31 as separately\n  generated 32-bit row registers with constant register-number write\n  comparisons and individual synchronous write enables. Do not use a\n  dynamically indexed storage array that Gowin can map back into RAM.\n  x0 is a constant zero row, never writable. Reset every stored row to\n  zero on the existing reset edge, preserving the current reset contract.\n\n  Build both asynchronous read ports as balanced bank-local masked OR\n  networks. Decode the low three source-address bits into eight mutually\n  exclusive row selects; form each bank's 32-bit output by OR-reducing\n  its eight masked rows. Decode the upper two bits into four mutually\n  exclusive bank selects and combine the four masked bank outputs.\n  Use explicit balanced reductions rather than a priority chain, and\n  keep write decoding local to each bank. The desired structure is\n  flip-flop clock-to-Q, a shallow local read network and the existing\n  ID operand register,\n…[2430 of 5502 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4afb83562a8bb1de]…\ne.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every current register-file assertion. Add deterministic\n      randomized read/write checks against a 32-register model, all\n      row/bank boundary combinations, both read ports accessing one row,\n      writes to x0, simultaneous bypass on both ports, and reset after\n      nonzero writes to all banks. Check write enable and unselected-row\n      preservation without depending on internal storage signal names.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing pipeline, prediction, divider and repair\n      assertions. Add architectural cases using sources and destinations\n      across all four RF banks, immediate EX forwarding, WB-to-ID reads,\n      both-source collisions and a dependent instruction held through\n      dmem backpressure after its WB source disappears. Check exact\n      RVFI operands/results, no extra dependency bubbles, memory effects\n      and unique retirement order. Keep the current payload layouts.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
