{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml, json, jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r7s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-002-r7s1',\n'title':'Decouple fetch from backend stalls with a four-entry instruction queue',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 178.53 at 75.013 MHz and 4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its fetch PC advances only when ID/EX captures an instruction; load-use bubbles, data-memory waits and divider waits therefore waste otherwise ready instruction-bus cycles. With approximately 22 percent independent stalls on both buses, buffering complete fetched instructions can overlap those waits. LESSONS.md is absent. The structural history includes successful operand/bypass retiming and registered multiply recovery, while an earlier registered-redirect experiment lost throughput. Isolated probes in this hypothesis-generation session found that moving multiplication to MEM alone and retiming the WB result mux do not exceed the current fitness threshold. The proposed single structural angle is frontend/backend decoupling; the existing execution and prediction datapaths remain the champion's.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Insert a four-entry elastic instruction FIFO between the existing fetch predictor/PC and decode. Each entry holds the full instruction, its fetch PC and the exact predicted_taken bit used to advance the fetch PC. Fetch accepts io_imemData and advances the predicted PC whenever io_imemReady is high, the FIFO has capacity, and no redirect is being applied, independently of load-use, dmem and divider holds in the backend. Decode consumes only the oldest queued entry when ID/EX can advance without a flush. Independent fetch and consume events can occur on the same edge. This keeps one instruction issue and one retirement per cycle while buffering bus availability across backend stalls.\n\nImplement a four-slot circular queue with two-bit head/tail pointers and a three-bit occupancy count. Empty entries are never valid; full queues never overwrite live entries. Permit enqueue on a full queue only when a dequeue occurs on the same edge. For simultaneous full dequeue/enqueue, the pre-edge head instruction is consumed and the replacement is written at the old tail, with both pointers advancing and occupancy unchanged. Reset and redirect clear count and pointers with priority over enqueue/dequeue. Payload bits need not reset, but empty/cleared output validity must gate all instruction effects; no unknown bits may reach a valid retirement. Do not bypass a new bus instruction directly into an empty queue's decode input in this first implementation: use the queued register boundary consistently.\n\nThe fetch predictor remains in if_stage and sees the current bus instruction and fetch PC. Its accept now means successful FIFO enqueue, so a held or unready fetch cannot consume prediction or change PC. On enqueue preserve exactly that prediction with its PC and instruction. Decode, register-file read addresses, registered bypass preparation and load-use comparisons must all use the FIFO head instruction, never the unrelated current io_imemData. Qualify the hazard unit's instruction-available input with FIFO-head validity rather than io_imemReady, allowing already buffered instructions to issue during an instruction-bus stall. Dequeue only when head-valid, \"'!stall_id and !flush_id; a load-use bubble keeps the head queued, and a downstream hold retains both the ID/EX instruction and the next queue head.\n\nKeep current EX branch/JALR resolution, training, narrow multiply prediction, exact MEM validation, registered older multiply repair, divider cancellation, memory semantics and WB boundary. Every redirect, including older registered MUL repair, atomically discards all queued younger instructions and sets the fetch PC to the existing redirect target, with priority over fetch acceptance and consume. Suppress enqueue during redirect and prevent cleared head validity from creating a younger capture. The original redirecting instruction still advances and retires once. Correct branch/JAL predictions need no queue flush. Prediction training remains gated by actual EX advancement, and each queued instruction retains the direction actually consumed at its enqueue edge even if the predictor table later changes. Branches already in the queue are recovered by the existing predicted-versus-actual comparison. Outstanding divider cancellation and younger MEM quarantine retain the champion'\"'s older-repair priority.\n\nThe queue adds one fetch-to-decode register boundary, so a misprediction or JALR pays a refill cycle and some queued predictions may use older counter state. These costs must be measured against saved bus stalls. Preserve the entire top-level I/O and nret=1 contract, decoder legality defaults, all real and ALTOPS arithmetic, memory alignment traps, original RVFI operands, correct next PCs and consecutive retirement orders. Do not alter stall generators, benchmark sources, timing wrappers, formal bounds or any evaluator. This is a structural fetch scheduling change, with no cache, new prediction policy or execution-arithmetic optimization.\n\nValidate FIFO empty/full/wraparound, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, reset and redirect under each occupancy. Pipeline tests must distinguish fetch acceptance from decode acceptance, track fetched prediction metadata through queue holds and wraparound, and check no drop, duplicate, reorder or wrong-path side effect. Cover dmem holds, load-use, independent imem stalls, divider waits, both branch directions, JAL/JALR, older multiply recovery and canceled younger divides. Retain all architectural assertions and exact arithmetic checks. Assess unchanged formal, ready/stalled full-trace cosim, canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs and bracketed cycles, and all three vendor Gowin placement options. Acceptance requires (Fmax_new / 75.013) * (4,201,709 / cycles_new) > 1.046. The expected gain is ten percent with medium confidence until the isolated probe completes; the main risks are FIFO mux/occupancy logic lowering Fmax and recovery/refill costs erasing the saved stalls.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/fetch_fifo.sv','description':'Add a four-entry circular FIFO carrying if_id_t PC, instruction and consumed prediction. Implement head/tail/count, valid/space, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue including full replacement, and reset/redirect priority. Payload cannot be observed as valid after clearing. Keep no combinational empty bypass.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Separate raw fetch_w from queued if_id_w. Drive fetch acceptance from io_imemReady and FIFO capacity; connect decode and register-file preparation to FIFO head. Consume on head-valid and advancing nonflushed ID. Flush the complete FIFO on existing redirect, including registered older MUL repair. Use head instruction fields for load-use and head validity for backend instruction availability. Keep all external ports, execution wiring, recovery priority and retirement unchanged.'},\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Clarify and enforce accept as FIFO enqueue rather than ID/EX capture. Preserve current instruction-qualified predictor, training and target logic; redirect overrides enqueue and all PC holds. Raw fetched bundle carries the exact PC/instruction/direction for enqueue, while backend holds no longer prevent a ready fetch with available space.'},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Treat frontend availability as queued-head valid rather than raw bus readiness. Preserve load-use bubble and downstream hold semantics using queued source fields, and existing redirect/divider/dmem priority. Empty queue advances an invalid ID payload while older instructions drain. Do not let io_imemReady invalidate a buffered head.'},\n{'file':'test/test_fetch_fifo.py','description':'Add directed queue tests for empty/full, wraparound, independent producer/consumer stalls, full simultaneous pop/push, capacity/count invariants, ordering and prediction/PC pairing. Exercise reset/redirect with pending push/pop at every occupancy and require no stale valid output.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Update timing-specific frontend scoreboard to distinguish fetch enqueue from ID dequeue, with a queue of exact PC/instruction/prediction tuples. Preserve arithmetic, traps, RVFI ordering, bypass, memory holds and recovery assertions. Exercise instruction stalls while buffered work issues, filling during backend waits, load-use head retention, all control transfers and older multiply repair at varying occupancies. Check single retirement and no wrong-path memory effects or uncanceled divides; retain architectural coverage for real and ALTOPS products.'},\n]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated schema-valid structural hypothesis',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
