{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r8s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Pipeline branch training separately from immediate control-flow recovery\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 188.51 at median 79.208 MHz and 4,201,709\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts at the MEM/WB\n  load result, crosses WB and forwarded EX branch resolution, and ends at\n  a direction-predictor counter. LESSONS.md is absent. Structural history\n  shows that registering all redirects lost throughput, whereas retiming\n  bypass decisions and registering multiply recovery improved fitness.\n  The eight-entry fetch queue reduced cycles but lost too much Fmax. The\n  remaining predictor-training feedback couples branch execution to table\n  lookup, saturating-counter update and distributed write enables in one\n  cycle. Splitting this feedback path can improve every instruction's\n  clock period without adding a branch-recovery cycle or an issue stage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert one register boundary between EX's qualified branch-resolution\n  event and the existing 64-entry two-bit direction-counter updater.\n  Capture a training record containing valid, the six-bit branch PC index,\n  and actual taken direction on every clock. Its valid bit is the current\n  branch_train_en, which already qualifies legal aligned conditional\n  branches by actual EX advancement, downstream acceptance, and absence\n  of an older multiply repair or divider wait. Update direction_q only\n  from the previous cycle's registered record. All counter-table reads,\n  saturating arithmetic, index decoding and write enables must use that\n  record, with no combinational current-EX operand, branch condition,\n  redirect or memory-ready dependency in the training write cone.\n\n  Implement the boundary local