{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category')=='structural' and 'r1s1' not in r['id']:\n  print(r['id'],r['title'])\n  h=r['hypothesis']; print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-002-r2s1 Register control-flow redirects at the EX/MEM boundary\nMove all taken BRANCH, JAL and JALR redirects from combinational EX outputs\nto the registered EX/MEM boundary. Resolve forwarded operands, branch\nconditions, targets, link values and alignment faults in EX exactly as\ntoday, but capture the redirect-valid bit and architectural next PC with\nthe instruction entering EX/MEM. Drive fetch redirect and younger-stage\ncancellation solely from those registers. Reuse the registered pc_next,\nbranch_taken and jump/trap controls if that avoids duplicate state. There\nmust be no combinational path from the current EX operand comparators or\nJALR adder into fetch redirect, instruction masking or frontend enables.\n\nThis changes control-transfer latency rather than instruction throughput:\nstraight-line code still issues one instruction per ready cycle, while\neach taken transfer incurs one additional recovery cycle. In the cycle\nwhen a registered redirect is applied, the branch/jump in EX/MEM must\nadvance normally to MEM/WB and retire once. Simultaneously kill the younger\ninstruction currently in EX, clear ID/EX, invalidate the fetched payload,\nand install the registered target PC. Suppress every launch or side effect\nof that younger EX instruction, especially divider start, divider payload\ncapture and EX/MEM capture; a wrong-path DIV must never make the redirect\nwait for arithmetic completion. A registered redirect must override\nload-use, imem-not-ready and a younger divider's tentative wait. Preserve\nexisting downstream memory transaction holds and WB forwarding retention.\nOnly an instruction that actually advances out of EX may create a pending\nredirect; an older stalled load/store must not cause a held branch to be\nlost, applied early or applied more than once. Misaligned or illegal\ncontrol transfers retain their existing traps and produce no redirect.\nA redirecting branch cannot legitimately have a younger divider already\nrunning in this in-order pipeline: suppressing launch during its recovery\ncycle is\n…[33059 of 36131 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:98100fbcd038171a]…\noards to the extra OC/EX register, and extend\nthem to check resolved-source retention and recovery of both younger\nstages. Exercise back-to-back dependent ALU chains at one issue per\nready cycle, nearest-writer priority, load-to-every-consumer-class,\nlong divider waits with an unrelated retiring WB source, bus holds,\nexact MUL repair, canceled divides and all branch/jump/trap cases.\n\nRun unchanged formal and ready/stalled full-trace cosim, canonical\nCoreMark CRCs/validation literal/MMIO brackets, and all three vendor\nGowin placements. Acceptance requires\n(Fmax_new / 79.208) * (4,201,709 / cycles_new) > 1.046.\nWith a five-percent cycle increase, median Fmax must exceed about\n87.0 MHz; 92 MHz would yield about ten percent more fitness. These are\ndesign targets, not measurements of this pipeline. Added registers,\nEX-to-OC return routing, the MEM load bypass and later recovery can\nerase the gain. The complete OC/EX split has not been implemented or\ntimed; the isolated smaller probes are not acceptance evidence.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The new operand stage raised fitness to 203.36, and the worst path now runs from data memory through load formatting and forwarding into operand capture. I’ll focus on removing that same-cycle load bypass and forwarding load results from registered WB instead. The tradeoff is one extra cycle for load dependencies; I’ll check whether the timing gain can cover it."}}
