ctions with no\nout-of-bounds access and the canonical CRCs. There were 93,960 MULs\nbetween the timing markers. The current signed-16-bit prediction\nmatches all of them; signed-nine-bit prediction matches 71,700 and\nmisses 22,260. This is an ISS operand profile, not an RTL cycle or\ntiming measurement. The additional misses must be included in the\nstalled CoreMark cycle count. A nominal three extra cycles per miss\nadds 66,780 cycles, about 1.59 percent; actual replay and bus-stall\noverlap must be measured. A materially faster native narrow DSP path\ncould exc\n…[12748 of 15820 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6182bb36a85d4db0]…\nrities. Aliases affect accuracy only because\ntargets are always calculated from the actual instruction's immediate.\n\nThe expected six-percent fitness gain has low confidence. A reduced\nlookup/update network may improve placement and clock frequency, and\nspreading high-PC aliases can reduce recovery cycles despite the smaller\ntable. XOR delay, reduced capacity and the existing EX/MEM-to-OC limits\nare the main risks. Profile direction errors and compare actual stalled\nbracketed cycles before assuming the new index is beneficial. Require\n(Fmax_new/91.186)*(4196601/cycles_new) > 1.046 for acceptance.\n\nPreserve nret=1, every external port, decoder defaults and reserved\nencoding traps, exact arithmetic and ALTOPS behavior, actual RVFI\noperands/results/next-PC/order, memory transactions and every invariant.\nRetain all existing architectural assertions and run unchanged formal,\nunit tests, ready/stalled trace cosim, canonical 2K CoreMark CRC/MMIO\nbracket/completion/bounded-access validation and median Gowin timing.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
