{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"agent_message","text":"A stronger candidate is narrow multiply-result prediction: all 93,960 CoreMark MULs matched a signed 16×16 product. Gowin also confirmed that a full-width multiplier lies on the current worst path.\n\nI’ll make that the final hypothesis: produce the narrow prediction in EX, verify the full-width result in MEM, and squash younger work on a mismatch. This targets Fmax while preserving correct execution for wide operands."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_33", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r3s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261005-003-r3s2\ntitle: \\\"Narrow multiply-result prediction with full-width MEM validation\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 117.75 at median Fmax 49.474 MHz and 4,201,709\n  counted CoreMark cycles. Both prior predictor attempts were reviewed in\n  log.jsonl; LESSONS.md is absent. The current 64-entry bimodal predictor\n  already removes most taken-control bubbles. A new read-only architectural\n  profile found 135,320 immediate load-to-branch dependencies, too few for\n  their removal alone to exceed the 184,779-cycle acceptance requirement at\n  unchanged Fmax. Improving branch direction alone has still less headroom.\n  A fresh unchanged-core Gowin option-0 run reproduces 50.610 MHz and the\n  25.054 ns worst arrival path: register-file BRAM output through ID operand\n  selection, EX forwarding, a full-width DSP multiplier and ALU selection,\n  then the data-memory address port. This is a physical timed path even\n  though multiplication instructions do not perform data-memory accesses.\n  The canonical CoreMark ELF executes 93,960 MULs between the MMIO markers;\n  every one has the same low-32-bit result as a signed 16-by-16 product.\n  There are 35,640 immediate raw source dependencies after these MULs.\n  These observations favor computational value prediction over a larger\n  branch predictor: shorten the timed EX product path while preserving\n  single-cycle predicted-result forwarding for the common operand range.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one stateless computational value predictor for multiplication.\n  In EX, use the fully forwarded operands to predict ALU_MUL as the 32-bit\n  signed product of a[15:0] and b[15:0], explicitly declared as two signed\n  16-bit inputs and a signed 32-bit output. This prediction deliberately\n  discards the upper operand bits; it is speculation, never an assumption\n  about legal RV32M operand values. Its result is exact when both operands\n  are sign-extended 16-bit values, including negative operands. For the\n  other multiply-family operations MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, predict zero initially\n  and use the same validation and recovery mechanism. The current workload\n  contains only MUL, but every legal high-product operation must remain\n  architecturally correct for arbitrary operands and instruction streams.\n  Do not add a result-history table, operand tags, a return predictor, or\n  another branch-prediction policy. Retain the existing bimodal/JAL logic.\n\n  Move full-width multiply evaluation onto the EX/MEM-to-MEM/WB path.\n  EX/MEM already carries the actual fully forwarded rs1_val, rs2_val,\n  operation, destination and instruction, so reuse those registered fields\n  as the exact multiplier inputs. Store the predicted product in its\n  alu_result field, making existing EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding deliver the\n  prediction to the immediately following instruction. In MEM, compute\n  the exact existing RV32M product variant from the registered operands,\n  compare it with the captured prediction, and always write the exact\n  product into MEM/WB. The retirement stage therefore commits the true\n  result and original operands even after an incorrect prediction.\n  Neither predicted values nor predictor correctness determine rvfi_trap.\n  Keep the full architectural 32-bit operands; do not narrow RVFI data.\n\n  On a mismatch for a valid multiplication that writes a nonzero rd,\n  generate an older-priority MEM repair to that multiplication's stored\n  PC+4. Preserve and retire the multiplication itself once with its exact\n  result. Flush the younger ID/EX instruction and current fetch, inject an\n  EX/MEM bubble instead of the younger EX result, and refetch the younger\n  instruction. Suppress that younger instruction's branch redirect,\n  direction-table training, divider start, and any other EX side effect\n  on the repair cycle. The younger instruction must never issue a memory\n  request, update architectural registers, or retire using a wrong product.\n  An older multiply's validation failure outranks every younger EX redirect\n  and frontend hazard. Skip recovery for rd=x0, whose speculative value\n  cannot be consumed. A correct prediction permits the younger instruction\n  to advance without a bubble; it continues to use the ordinary forwarding\n  priority. Use the registered EX/MEM pc_next for the repair target, which\n  is PC+4 for multiplication, to avoid an adder after the product comparator.\n\n  Keep the exact multiplier physically separated from EX's ordinary ALU\n  result selection. Add a constant ALU parameter, default enabled for the\n  standalone ALU tests, that disables its full-width multiplication cone\n  in the EX instance. This avoids relying on synthesis to eliminate a\n  full-width product hidden behind overlapping downstream opcode muxes.\n  Factor the existing exact four-product semantics into a local multiplier\n  helper reused by the standalone ALU and MEM validator. The EX instance\n  has only the narrow prediction for multiply-family results. Preserve\n  ADD/SUB/shifts, the shared iterative divider, all RV32M exceptions,\n  and the start/busy/result-valid/consume interface. This stage movement is\n  supporting machinery for validated value prediction, rather than a\n  separately interlocked multiplier redesign.\n\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, both prediction and validation must use the\n  exact existing operation-specific add/sub-XOR formulas on the same\n  original operands. Keep all existing formal checks and bounds unchanged;\n  the ALTOPS configuration still exercises operand routing and normal\n  prediction acceptance. Directed real-arithmetic tests must also force\n  prediction failures, since narrow-product acceptance in CoreMark and\n  ALTOPS cannot validate the squash path. Do not modify any decoder legality\n  rule, the external ports, nret=1, RVFI order, memory contract, benchmarks,\n  or evaluation infrastructure. Keep load-use and bus/divider stall behavior\n  intact apart from the older-priority multiplication recovery signal.\n\n  Expect 10 percent higher fitness, with medium confidence. All 93,960\n  profiled benchmark MUL predictions matched, so the architectural profile\n  suggests no new counted bubbles on CoreMark; this remains a profile,\n  not an implemented pipeline or measured candidate fitness. The expected\n  gain comes from using a smaller DSP operation before EX/MEM and feeding\n  full-width validation from registered operands instead of the BRAM-read\n  and forwarding chain. The risks are a new MEM-product/comparison/recovery\n  critical path, extra DSP/register routing, and wide-operand recovery cost\n  on other workloads. Check actual DSP inference rather than assuming the\n  narrow expression maps well. Require unchanged formal and exact RV32M\n  tests, stalled CoreMark cosim and canonical CRCs, and median Gowin Fmax.\n  Acceptance requires (Fmax_new / 49.474 MHz) * (4,201,709 / cycles_new)\n  greater than 1.046. With unchanged cycles, median Fmax must exceed\n  51.750 MHz. Preserve every hard invariant and every existing test.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mul_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the stateless signed 16-by-16 low-product predictor and a local\n      exact full-width multiplier helper for all four RV32M product variants.\n      Use explicit widths and signedness. Predict zero for real high-product\n      operations. Preserve the exact operation-specific ALTOPS substitutes\n      in both prediction and validation. No benchmark addresses or constants.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Factor exact multiplication into the helper and add an enable parameter\n      whose default preserves all standalone ALU results. Disable the exact\n      multiplication cone only in the EX instance. Leave divider arithmetic,\n      handshake, ALTOPS behavior, and every base integer operation intact.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use narrow predicted multiply results in EX/MEM and retain the original\n      fully forwarded source operands for MEM validation. Add an older-repair\n      squash input that prevents younger EX/MEM transfer, branch repair,\n      predictor training and divider launch, without dropping the older\n      validating instruction or changing any existing divider transaction.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Evaluate and select exact full-width multiplication from EX/MEM's\n      registered operands before MEM/WB capture. Export one-shot mismatch\n      repair on valid advancement for nonzero destinations, using stored\n      pc_next as PC+4. Keep original RVFI source data, actual rd result and\n      single retirement; retain memory alignment, masks and stall semantics.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Arbitrate older MEM multiplication recovery ahead of younger EX recovery,\n      feed the selected repair to existing fetch/hazard flush controls, and\n      suppress younger EX effects. Keep EX/MEM predicted-result forwarding\n      and MEM/WB exact-result forwarding correctly ordered. Preserve all ports.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every exact base/MUL/DIV/REM vector and confirm the default ALU\n      still returns exact full-width products. Add predictor/helper checks\n      for signed 16-bit boundaries, zero and negative factors, full-width\n      inputs that invalidate narrow predictions, and all high-product variants.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain current tests and add narrow-product matches plus forced wide\n      mismatches, each followed by dependent arithmetic, loads, stores,\n      branches, JALR or DIV/REM. Exercise consecutive multiply predictions,\n      rd=x0, high-product variants, forwarded and loaded operands, and bus\n      stalls at advancement/recovery. Check exact RVFI operands/results,\n      unique retirement, no wrong-path memory effects or divider launch,\n      older-repair priority, correct replay PC and bubble-free matches.\n''')\nprint('Replaced provisional hypothesis with narrow multiply-result value prediction.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_33", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r3s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261005-003-r3s2\ntitle: \\\"Narrow multiply-result prediction with full-width MEM validation\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 117.75 at median Fmax 49.474 MHz and 4,201,709\n  counted CoreMark cycles. Both prior predictor attempts were reviewed in\n  log.jsonl; LESSONS.md is absent. The current 64-entry bimodal predictor\n  already removes most taken-control bubbles. A new read-only architectural\n  profile found 135,320 immediate load-to-branch dependencies, too few for\n  their removal alone to exceed the 184,779-cycle acceptance requirement at\n  unchanged Fmax. Improving branch direction alone has still less headroom.\n  A fresh unchanged-core Gowin option-0 run reproduces 50.610 MHz and the\n  25.054 ns worst arrival path: register-file BRAM output through ID operand\n  selection, EX forwarding, a full-width DSP multiplier and ALU selection,\n  then the data-memory address port. This is a physical timed path even\n  though multiplication instructions do not perform data-memory accesses.\n  The canonical CoreMark ELF executes 93,960 MULs between the MMIO markers;\n  every one has the same low-32-bit result as a signed 16-by-16 product.\n  There are 35,640 immediate raw source dependencies after these MULs.\n  These observations favor computational value prediction over a larger\n  branch predictor: shorten the timed EX product path while preserving\n  single-cycle predicted-result forwarding for the common operand range.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one stateless computational value predictor for multiplication.\n  In EX, use the fully forwarded operands to predict ALU_MUL as the 32-bit\n  signed product of a[15:0] and b[15:0], explicitly declared as two signed\n  16-bit inputs and a signed 32-bit output. This prediction deliberately\n  discards the upper operand bits; it is speculation, never an assumption\n  about legal RV32M operand values. Its result is exact when both operands\n  are sign-extended 16-bit values, including negative operands. For the\n  other multiply-family operations MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, predict zero initially\n  and use the same validation and recovery mechanism. The current workload\n  contains only MUL, but every legal high-product operation must remain\n  architecturally correct for arbitrary operands and instruction streams.\n  Do not add a result-history table, operand tags, a return predictor, or\n  another branch-prediction policy. Retain the existing bimodal/JAL logic.\n\n  Move full-width multiply evaluation onto the EX/MEM-to-MEM/WB path.\n  EX/MEM already carries the actual fully forwarded rs1_val, rs2_val,\n  operation, destination and instruction, so reuse those registered fields\n  as the exact multiplier inputs. Store the predicted product in its\n  alu_result field, making existing EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding deliver the\n  prediction to the immediately following instruction. In MEM, compute\n  the exact existing RV32M product variant from the registered operands,\n  compare it with the captured prediction, and always write the exact\n  product into MEM/WB. The retirement stage therefore commits the true\n  result and original operands even after an incorrect prediction.\n  Neither predicted values nor predictor correctness determine rvfi_trap.\n  Keep the full architectural 32-bit operands; do not narrow RVFI data.\n\n  On a mismatch for a valid multiplication that writes a nonzero rd,\n  generate an older-priority MEM repair to that multiplication's stored\n  PC+4. Preserve and retire the multiplication itself once with its exact\n  result. Flush the younger ID/EX instruction and current fetch, inject an\n  EX/MEM bubble instead of the younger EX result, and refetch the younger\n  instruction. Suppress that younger instruction's branch redirect,\n  direction-table training, divider start, and any other EX side effect\n  on the repair cycle. The younger instruction must never issue a memory\n  request, update architectural registers, or retire using a wrong product.\n  An older multiply's validation failure outranks every younger EX redirect\n  and frontend hazard. Skip recovery for rd=x0, whose speculative value\n  cannot be consumed. A correct prediction permits the younger instruction\n  to advance without a bubble; it continues to use the ordinary forwarding\n  priority. Use the registered EX/MEM pc_next for the repair target, which\n  is PC+4 for multiplication, to avoid an adder after the product comparator.\n\n  Keep the exact multiplier physically separated from EX's ordinary ALU\n  result selection. Add a constant ALU parameter, default enabled for the\n  standalone ALU tests, that disables its full-width multiplication cone\n  in the EX instance. This avoids relying on synthesis to eliminate a\n  full-width product hidden behind overlapping downstream opcode muxes.\n  Factor the existing exact four-product semantics into a local multiplier\n  helper reused by the standalone ALU and MEM validator. The EX instance\n  has only the narrow prediction for multiply-family results. Preserve\n  ADD/SUB/shifts, the shared iterative divider, all RV32M exceptions,\n  and the start/busy/result-valid/consume interface. This stage movement is\n  supporting machinery for validated value prediction, rather than a\n  separately interlocked multiplier redesign.\n\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, both prediction and validation must use the\n  exact existing operation-specific add/sub-XOR formulas on the same\n  original operands. Keep all existing formal checks and bounds unchanged;\n  the ALTOPS configuration still exercises operand routing and normal\n  prediction acceptance. Directed real-arithmetic tests must also force\n  prediction failures, since narrow-product acceptance in CoreMark and\n  ALTOPS cannot validate the squash path. Do not modify any decoder legality\n  rule, the external ports, nret=1, RVFI order, memory contract, benchmarks,\n  or evaluation infrastructure. Keep load-use and bus/divider stall behavior\n  intact apart from the older-priority multiplication recovery signal.\n\n  Expect 10 percent higher fitness, with medium confidence. All 93,960\n  profiled benchmark MUL predictions matched, so the architectural profile\n  suggests no new counted bubbles on CoreMark; this remains a profile,\n  not an implemented pipeline or measured candidate fitness. The expected\n  gain comes from using a smaller DSP operation before EX/MEM and feeding\n  full-width validation from registered operands instead of the BRAM-read\n  and forwarding chain. The risks are a new MEM-product/comparison/recovery\n  critical path, extra DSP/register routing, and wide-operand recovery cost\n  on other workloads. Check actual DSP inference rather than assuming the\n  narrow expression maps well. Require unchanged formal and exact RV32M\n  tests, stalled CoreMark cosim and canonical CRCs, and median Gowin Fmax.\n  Acceptance requires (Fmax_new / 49.474 MHz) * (4,201,709 / cycles_new)\n  greater than 1.046. With unchanged cycles, median Fmax must exceed\n  51.750 MHz. Preserve every hard invariant and every existing test.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mul_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the stateless signed 16-by-16 low-product predictor and a local\n      exact full-width multiplier helper for all four RV32M product variants.\n      Use explicit widths and signedness. Predict zero for real high-product\n      operations. Preserve the exact operation-specific ALTOPS substitutes\n      in both prediction and validation. No benchmark addresses or constants.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Factor exact multiplication into the helper and add an enable parameter\n      whose default preserves all standalone ALU results. Disable the exact\n      multiplication cone only in the EX instance. Leave divider arithmetic,\n      handshake, ALTOPS behavior, and every base integer operation intact.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use narrow predicted multiply results in EX/MEM and retain the original\n      fully forwarded source operands for MEM validation. Add an older-repair\n      squash input that prevents younger EX/MEM transfer, branch repair,\n      predictor training and divider launch, without dropping the older\n      validating instruction or changing any existing divider transaction.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Evaluate and select exact full-width multiplication from EX/MEM's\n      registered operands before MEM/WB capture. Export one-shot mismatch\n      repair on valid advancement for nonzero destinations, using stored\n      pc_next as PC+4. Keep original RVFI source data, actual rd result and\n      single retirement; retain memory alignment, masks and stall semantics.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Arbitrate older MEM multiplication recovery ahead of younger EX recovery,\n      feed the selected repair to existing fetch/hazard flush controls, and\n      suppress younger EX effects. Keep EX/MEM predicted-result forwarding\n      and MEM/WB exact-result forwarding correctly ordered. Preserve all ports.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every exact base/MUL/DIV/REM vector and confirm the default ALU\n      still returns exact full-width products. Add predictor/helper checks\n      for signed 16-bit boundaries, zero and negative factors, full-width\n      inputs that invalidate narrow predictions, and all high-product variants.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain current tests and add narrow-product matches plus forced wide\n      mismatches, each followed by dependent arithmetic, loads, stores,\n      branches, JALR or DIV/REM. Exercise consecutive multiply predictions,\n      rd=x0, high-product variants, forwarded and loaded operands, and bus\n      stalls at advancement/recovery. Check exact RVFI operands/results,\n      unique retirement, no wrong-path memory effects or divider launch,\n      older-repair priority, correct replay PC and bubble-free matches.\n''')\nprint('Replaced provisional hypothesis with narrow multiply-result value prediction.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replaced provisional hypothesis with narrow multiply-result value prediction.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
